{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018qNHvNwPk3eTUcPdhNSwET","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VbgWNAGj2H8SxuS9QLLX8F","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods+Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas: **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n# Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination in LLM Agent Trajectories\"\n\n**Core idea:** LLM agents frequently enter \"semantic stagnation\" — executing diverse but collectively ineffective actions. The paper proposes a framework for real-time stagnation monitoring using 30 lightweight structural features (computed in <5ms per window), bridging detection to early termination intervention.\n\n## Method (Section 3)\n- Trajectories partitioned into overlapping windows of k=5 steps with stride 1\n- Three labeling functions (LFs) in a weak supervision pipeline:\n  - **cmd_rep**: Fires when command repetition rate exceeds threshold. High precision (97.9%) but agent-specific coverage (65.5% SWE-Agent vs 5.4% OpenHands)\n  - **file_diff**: Fires when no file modifications detected. Cross-agent stable (17.8% on OH)\n  - **outcome_backpropagation (OB)**: Propagates task outcome backward as distant supervision — requires final outcome, unavailable at inference time\n- Silver labels via majority vote (MV) over LF outputs. Confidence conf ∈ {1,2,3} = number of agreeing LFs\n- 2-LF deployable config (cmd_rep + file_diff, no OB): 0.978 F1 at conf≥2, 100% label agreement with 3-LF on shared conf≥2 subset\n- 30 structural features: command patterns, file activity, output statistics, temporal signals, error indicators\n- Gradient Boosting (GB) and Random Forest (RF) classifiers on SWE-Agent silver labels (conf≥2)\n- GRU probes on frozen embeddings from 3 encoders (Qwen3-0.6B, BGE-M3, E5-large-v2)\n- Early termination policy: terminate when k consecutive windows predicted as stagnation\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n\n### 4.1 Anti-Circularity Validation on Gold Labels (N=505)\n- Gold set: 3 annotators, 505 windows, Fleiss κ=0.39\n- GB achieves 0.839 F1 on gold, exceeding MV silver labels (0.798) and weakest annotator (0.831)\n- Human ceiling (Spearman-Brown adjusted): 0.870; GB gap = -3.1pp\n- Conf=1 Error Decomposition (282 windows where LFs disagree):\n  - GB F1=0.784 vs MV agreement 73.8% (p<0.01)\n  - Beyond-LF detection rate: 63.2% (48/76 MV errors corrected by GB)\n  - Table 1: GB✓MV✗=48 (17%), Both✓=176 (62.4%), Both✗=28 (9.9%), GB✗MV✓=30 (10.6%)\n- Power analysis: N=505 detects ≥4pp at power=0.82. GB vs MV (4.2pp) significant; TWV-Probe vs GB (-2.3pp, p=0.056) borderline\n\n### 4.2 Matched LLM Comparison on OpenHands\n- Structural GB (OH): 0.967 macro-F1\n- Best LLM (Claude few-shot-CoT on OH): 0.701 → gap 26.6pp\n- LLM transfer degradation SWE→OH: 7.8–10.9pp; structural only degrades 2.8pp\n- Table 4: 5 methods compared on identical OH data (N=2000 stratified sample)\n\n### 4.3 Encoder-Independence\n- Three encoders all degrade 31–33pp under cross-agent transfer\n- Gap to structural: 34.6–36.9pp (mean 35.8±1.2pp)\n- Confirms signal-type difference (structural vs semantic), not encoder capability\n\n### 4.4 Compute Waste Analysis\n- 5,092 trajectories (1,683 SWE + 3,409 OH)\n- 52.4% of steps, 56.3% of tokens occur after first stagnation onset\n- 78.6% of trajectories have ≥1 stagnation tail\n\n### 4.5 Early Termination Simulation\n- k=3 recommended: 35.3% compute saved, 95.5% task success preserved, 3.8% false termination rate\n- Pareto frontier: structural AUC 0.891 vs oracle 0.952 vs random 0.623\n\n### 4.6 2-LF Coverage\n- SWE-Agent: 18.7% conf≥2 coverage; OpenHands: only 3.6%\n- Low OH coverage due to cmd_rep's 5.4% fire rate\n\n### 4.7 Calibration\n- GB ECE raw 0.061 → tempcal 0.045 (silver), 0.083 → 0.048 (gold)\n- Temperature T=2.06; predictions highly bimodal\n\n### Section 5 Analysis\n- SHAP decomposition: LF-overlap 40.1%, Partial-overlap 26.0%, Residual (beyond-LF) 33.9%\n- Three qualitative trajectory examples\n- Stride sensitivity: GB robust (<0.4pp); probe degrades 2.4pp at stride 5\n\n### Section 6: Temporal Label Model — Negative Finding\n- Three TLM variants (TWV, HMM-TLM, Lag-aware HMM) all fail to improve over MV\n- Attributed to low-conflict regime: conf≥2 windows have 95.1% consistency with gold\n\n### Section 7: Limitations\n- Agent scope: only SE agents (SWE-Agent, OpenHands)\n- Gold set size: N=505 underpowered for <3pp effects\n- 2-LF OH coverage: 3.6%\n- Simulation vs online deployment gap\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe paper covers all 12 imagined experiments from the design doc:\n1. conf1-gold-error-decomposition ✓\n2. llm-on-oh (matched comparison) ✓\n3. cross-agent-probe-bge ✓\n4. cross-agent-probe-e5 ✓\n5. compute-waste-audit ✓\n6. early-termination-simulation ✓\n7. cost-effectiveness-pareto ✓\n8. 2lf-coverage-analysis ✓\n9. calibration-analysis ✓\n10. gold-power-analysis ✓\n11. human-ceiling-estimate ✓\n12. stride-sensitivity ✓\nPlus: feature-importance (SHAP), trajectory visualization, temporal label model negative result\n\n---\n\n# Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness** — Is the problem formulation clean? Are the labeling functions well-designed? Is the weak supervision pipeline appropriate? Are there methodological gaps or flaws?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor** — Are experiments well-designed and sufficient to support the claims? Are baselines adequate? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are there confounds?\n\n3. **Anti-Circularity Analysis** — The paper acknowledges that structural features overlap with LF inputs (40% SHAP from LF-overlap features). Is the gold validation sufficient to address circularity? Is the conf=1 analysis convincing?\n\n4. **Reproducibility** — Are details sufficient for reproduction?\n\n5. **Specific Concerns** — Any methodological issues, missing experiments, or questionable claims.\n\n6. **Strengths** — What does the paper do well methodologically/experimentally?\n\nFormat your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Missing Experiments or Analyses\n- Minor Issues\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (1-5)\n- Score justification (2-3 sentences)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15038,"cache_read_input_tokens":44246,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15038},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3","uuid":"06b43caa-d3ff-4826-a30e-1e3690cd4d5c"}
