{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UvVNCiyfwfecHbi5Uejb6H","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\tWe organize results around three questions:\n5\t(1)~which method best detects stagnation across deployment scenarios? (\\S\\ref{sec:main_results}--\\S\\ref{sec:robustness});\n6\t(2)~what drives label quality and cross-coding-agent transfer bottlenecks? (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}--\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation});\n7\t(3)~can temporal structure in LFs improve labels? (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_label_models}).\n8\tAll reported metrics are evaluated against silver labels derived from majority vote over three labeling functions (\\S\\ref{sec:method}).\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{Circular evaluation caveat.}\n11\tStructural features (command repetition ratios, file operation counts, edit distances) are computed from the same signals that the labeling functions use to produce silver labels.\n12\tConsequently, the in-domain structural F1 of 0.995 reflects an upper bound on how well these features can \\emph{reverse-engineer} the labeling functions, not detection accuracy against true ground truth.\n13\tThe cross-coding-agent evaluation on OpenHands (0.967 macro-F1) is non-circular: models trained on SWE-Agent LF distributions are tested on a different agent whose LF firing patterns differ substantially (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n14\tA human-annotated gold set provides additional non-circular evidence (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}).\n15\t\n16\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n17\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n18\t\\label{sec:setup}\n19\t\n20\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}\n21\tSliding windows of $k{=}5$ consecutive actions are extracted from SWE-Agent (54,809 windows, 28.5\\% stagnation) \\citep{jimenez2024swebench,yang2024sweagent} and OpenHands (150,652 windows, 20.9\\% stagnation) \\citep{wang2025openhands} trajectories on SWE-bench.\n22\tSilver labels are produced by majority vote; confidence equals the count of agreeing LFs (1--3).\n23\tSplits are at the trajectory level (70/15/15\\%); evaluation uses conf${\\geq}$2: $N{=}1{,}824$ (SWE-Agent) and $N{=}43{,}376$ (OH).\n24\t\n25\t\\paragraph{Detection methods.}\n26\t(1)~\\textbf{Structural features}: 30 hand-crafted features (repetition rates, file operation counts, edit distances, output entropy) fed to Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and Gradient Boosting (GB).\n27\t(2)~\\textbf{ProgressProbe}: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B \\citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} embeddings (1024-dim) processed by a 2-layer GRU \\citep{cho2014gru} with attention \\citep{bahdanau2015attention} (hidden$=$256, dropout$=$0.1), early-stopped at epoch~3 with patience~5.\n28\t(3)~\\textbf{Mean-pool + LR}: Mean-pooled Qwen3 embeddings with logistic regression.\n29\t(4)~\\textbf{LLM-as-Judge}: Claude Sonnet 4.6 zero-shot window-level classification (\\$11.66 API cost for the SWE test set).\n30\t(5)~\\textbf{Cosine similarity}: Cosine distance between consecutive window embeddings with a validation-tuned threshold.\n31\t\n32\t\\paragraph{Metrics.}\n33\tMacro-F1 is the primary metric. We also report per-class F1 for stagnation (F1\\textsubscript{stag}) and progress (F1\\textsubscript{prog}).\n34\t\n35\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n36\t\\subsection{Main Results}\n37\t\\label{sec:main_results}\n38\t\n39\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} presents in-domain results on the SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 test set and cross-coding-agent transfer to OpenHands.\n40\t\n41\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n42\t\\centering\n43\t\\small\n44\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc cc}\n45\t\\toprule\n46\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{SWE-Agent In-Domain} (conf${\\geq}$2)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{OpenHands Transfer}} \\\\\n47\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-6}\n48\t\\textbf{Method} & Macro-F1 & F1\\textsubscript{stag} & F1\\textsubscript{prog} & All & Conf${\\geq}$2 \\\\\n49\t\\midrule\n50\tStructural RF$^\\dagger$       & \\textbf{0.9951} & 0.9928 & 0.9974 & 0.9123 & \\textbf{0.9959} \\\\\n51\tStructural GB$^\\dagger$       & \\textbf{0.9951} & 0.9928 & 0.9974 & \\textbf{0.9674} & \\underline{0.9918} \\\\\n52\tStructural LR$^\\dagger$       & 0.9874 & 0.9815 & 0.9933 & 0.7801 & 0.9671 \\\\\n53\tProgressProbe (GRU)           & \\underline{0.9055} & 0.8593 & 0.9518 & 0.598$^a$ & 0.598$^a$ \\\\\n54\t\\quad + full OH fine-tune      & ---    & ---    & ---    & 0.976$^b$ & 0.976$^b$ \\\\\n55\tMean-pool + LR                & 0.8435 & 0.7620 & 0.9250 & ---    & --- \\\\\n56\tLLM-as-Judge                  & 0.6625 & 0.6055 & 0.7195 & ---    & --- \\\\\n57\tCosine Similarity             & 0.5606 & ---    & ---    & 0.447  & 0.393 \\\\\n58\t\\bottomrule\n59\t\\end{tabular}\n60\t\\caption{Stagnation detection results (SWE-Agent in-domain, $N{=}1{,}824$; OpenHands zero-shot transfer). $^\\dagger$Structural metrics reflect LF-reconstructibility, not ground-truth accuracy (\\S\\ref{sec:circular}). $^a$Probe evaluated on 15\\% test split ($N{=}6{,}122$); structural models on all OH windows ($N{=}150{,}652$) and conf${\\geq}$2 ($N{=}43{,}376$). $^b$Paired bootstrap ($N{=}6{,}122$): GB leads fine-tuned probe by 1.65\\,pp (CI [1.11, 2.23], $p{<}0.001$) on conf${\\geq}$2; probe advantage on all windows reflects better conf${=}1$ performance. \\textbf{Bold} = best, \\underline{underline} = second.}\n61\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n62\t\\end{table*}\n63\t\n64\tIn-domain, structural RF achieves 0.995 macro-F1, exceeding the GRU probe by 8.96\\,pp (paired bootstrap: 95\\% CI [7.44, 10.57]\\,pp, $p{<}0.001$).\n65\tHowever, the structural--probe gap is inflated by circular evaluation: structural features reconstruct the LFs that generated the labels.\n66\tThe more informative comparison is among non-circular methods: the GRU probe outperforms mean-pool~+~LR by 6.2\\,pp, confirming that temporal modeling within windows adds value beyond static embedding aggregation.\n67\tThe zero-shot LLM judge (0.663) underperforms all learned methods despite higher cost (\\$11.66 per test set).\n68\tExtended evaluation (Appendix~\\ref{sec:llm_extended}) yields a best LLM macro-F1 of 0.799 (five-shot-CoT), still 16.8\\,pp below structural cross-coding-agent transfer (0.967 on OpenHands)---the structural advantage holds even comparing the harder cross-coding-agent setting against the LLM's easier in-domain regime.\n69\tCoT and few-shot yield comparable gains ($+$8.7 vs.\\ $+$8.9\\,pp); combining them adds $+$4.8\\,pp.\n70\tZero-shot with structural features (0.688) improves over plain zero-shot by $+$2.6\\,pp but remains below few-shot (0.751), suggesting structural signal is partially accessible to LLMs but not exploitable as effectively as by trained classifiers.\n71\t\n72\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n73\t\\subsection{Cross-Agent Transfer}\n74\t\\label{sec:cross_agent}\n75\t\n76\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} (right columns) shows models trained on SWE-Agent and evaluated on OpenHands without any OH training data.\n77\tStructural GB transfers best, achieving 0.967 macro-F1 on all OH windows.\n78\tRF drops to 0.912 on all-confidence OH data despite matching GB in-domain, suggesting GB's boosted ensemble is more robust to distributional shift.\n79\tBoth structural models exceed 0.99 macro-F1 on conf${\\geq}$2 OH windows (GB 0.992, RF 0.996), where label noise is lower.\n80\t\n81\tThe probe's zero-shot transfer (0.598) lags structural GB by 36.9\\,pp---cosine similarity collapses further to 0.447 (mean cosine distance between stagnation and progress windows is 0.610 vs.\\ 0.613, showing essentially no class separation)---but full OH fine-tuning closes this gap dramatically: the fine-tuned probe reaches 0.976 macro-F1 on all OH windows, surpassing structural GB (0.967) by 0.9\\,pp (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n82\tThe asymmetry between structural and learned methods is thus stage-dependent: in the zero-data regime, structural features transfer naturally while learned embeddings do not (+36.9\\,pp gap); with target-domain fine-tuning, the probe surpasses structural features (+0.9\\,pp on all windows).\n83\tThe practical implication is a deployment trade-off: use structural features when no labeled data is available; invest in probe fine-tuning when annotation budget permits.\n84\tHowever, this crossover is scope-dependent: on conf${\\geq}$2 windows, where label noise is minimal, GB retains its advantage (0.992 vs.\\ 0.976; paired bootstrap $p{<}0.001$, $N{=}6{,}122$), suggesting that the probe's all-windows edge reflects better tolerance of noisy low-confidence labels rather than superior high-confidence detection.\n85\t\n86\t\\paragraph{Multi-seed stability.}\n87\tStructural RF: $0.995{\\pm}0.000$ in-domain (3 seeds); GB cross-coding-agent: $0.968{\\pm}0.002$.\n88\tThe probe shows higher variance ($0.919{\\pm}0.011$) but all seeds exceed 0.90.\n89\t\n90\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n91\t\\subsection{Robustness Analysis}\n92\t\\label{sec:robustness}\n93\t\n94\tThe probe is encoder-agnostic: three backbones all exceed 0.90 F1 (Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:encoder_ablation}), and a Transformer encoder is equivalent to GRU ($+$0.18\\,pp, $p{=}0.82$) on 5-step windows.\n95\tWindow size $k{=}5$ is optimal; $k{=}3$ drops 5.9\\,pp, $k{=}7$ drops 1.8\\,pp (Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:window_ablation}).\n96\tStructural classifiers are extremely data-efficient: RF achieves 0.991 and GB 0.987 at just 10\\% training data, fully saturating by 50\\%---a 20$\\times$ advantage over the probe (Figure~\\ref{fig:data_scaling}).\n97\t\n98\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n99\t\\centering\n100\t\\includegraphics[width=0.9\\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_data_scaling.pdf}\n101\t\\caption{Macro-F1 vs.\\ training data fraction. Structural classifiers saturate by 50\\%; the probe has not plateaued at 100\\%.}\n102\t\\label{fig:data_scaling}\n103\t\\end{figure}\n104\t\n105\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n106\t\\subsection{Labeling Function Temporal Dynamics}\n107\t\\label{sec:lf_temporal}\n108\t\n109\tThe three LFs occupy distinct temporal niches relative to stagnation onset (Table~\\ref{tab:lf_temporal}, Figure~\\ref{fig:lf_temporal} in Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_lf_temporal}): cmd\\_rep leads by 1.87 steps, file\\_diff is near-concurrent ($-1.20$), and outcome\\_bp lags by 6.70 steps (all pairwise $p{<}10^{-26}$).\n110\tOn OpenHands, cmd\\_rep reverses to lagging ($+3.32$ steps), firing on only 5.4\\% of windows with 97.9\\% precision---a high-precision, low-recall detector whose temporal role differs fundamentally from its SWE-Agent behavior.\n111\t\n112\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n113\t\\subsection{Label Quality and cmd\\_rep Coverage}\n114\t\\label{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}\n115\t\n116\tA label quality audit revealed a version mismatch in OH embedding vectors; after correction, the OH stagnation rate shifted from 6.56\\% to 21.4\\%, and 5\\% fine-tuning F1 improved from 0.570 to 0.850---demonstrating that label quality, not model capacity, was the transfer bottleneck (full curve in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:cross_agent_cmdrep}).\n117\t\n118\t\\begin{table}[t]\n119\t\\centering\n120\t\\small\n121\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n122\t\\toprule\n123\t\\textbf{LF Config} & \\textbf{Macro-F1} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{stag}} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{prog}} \\\\\n124\t\\midrule\n125\t3-LF (w/ cmd\\_rep) & \\textbf{0.9763} & 0.9600 & 0.9930 \\\\\n126\t2-LF (no cmd\\_rep) & 0.6822 & 0.4810 & 0.8840 \\\\\n127\t\\midrule\n128\t$\\Delta$ & $-29.4$\\,pp & $-47.9$\\,pp & $-10.9$\\,pp \\\\\n129\t\\bottomrule\n130\t\\end{tabular}\n131\t\\caption{Prevalence-matched ablation on the same OH test set ($N{=}6{,}122$, stag rate 19.3\\%). Removing cmd\\_rep drops macro-F1 by 29.4\\,pp (McNemar $\\chi^2{=}1551$, $p{\\approx}0$).}\n132\t\\label{tab:cmd_rep_ablation}\n133\t\\end{table}\n134\t\n135\tRemoving cmd\\_rep drops macro-F1 by 29.4\\,pp (Table~\\ref{tab:cmd_rep_ablation}; McNemar $p{\\approx}0$) due to data volume collapse: cmd\\_rep expands training data ${\\sim}$10$\\times$ (4K$\\to$43K windows) while agreeing 100\\% with 2-LF labels on overlapping windows---purely a coverage contribution (Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_label_quality}).\n136\t\n137\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n138\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Models}\n139\t\\label{sec:temporal_label_models}\n140\t\n141\tFive label models---three temporal (TWV, HMM, Lag-HMM) and two generative (FlyingSquid, Snorkel MeTaL)---all fail to improve over majority vote (Table~\\ref{tab:temporal_models} in Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_tlm_results}); the best (TWV, $+$0.6\\,pp self-evaluated) degrades downstream probe F1 by 2.57\\,pp.\n142\tThe bottleneck is LF quantity: three binary LFs produce at most $2^3{=}8$ voting patterns with insufficient inter-LF conflict to exploit.\n143\t\n144\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n145\t\\subsection{Gold Set Validation}\n146\t\\label{sec:gold_set}\n147\tThree CS graduate students annotate a stratified gold set; 512 windows received at least one annotation, with Set~B ($N{=}505$, ${\\geq}$2 annotators agree) as the primary evaluation (protocol and IAA in Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_gold_set}).\n148\tGB achieves 0.839 gold macro-F1 vs.\\ 0.798 for MV silver labels ($+$4.2\\,pp, paired bootstrap $p{<}0.001$), confirming that structural features detect stagnation beyond LF reconstruction.\n149\tProbe models are evaluated on the SWE-only subset ($N{\\approx}335$) due to missing OH embeddings; cross-method comparisons should account for differing $N$ (Table~\\ref{tab:gold_batch1})."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012NX7ee5aKMp69d7sggVgGk","session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3","uuid":"4adbda30-373a-4e51-b300-46d404ecaec0","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:12:34.235Z"}
