{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VbgWNAGj2H8SxuS9QLLX8F","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me compile my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural? A Systematic Empirical Study of Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision\"\n\n**Reviewer R1 (Methods + Experiments)**\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper studies whether structural features suffice to detect \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM coding agent trajectories. Using a weak supervision pipeline with three labeling functions over 205K+ sliding windows from SWE-Agent and OpenHands, the authors show that 30 hand-crafted structural features achieve strong cross-agent transfer (0.967 macro-F1) without target data, outperforming neural probes and LLM judges. A deployment-regime trade-off is identified: structural features dominate in zero-data settings, but a GRU probe surpasses them with target fine-tuning. Five temporal label models fail to improve over majority vote.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Honest and transparent treatment of circularity.** The paper proactively flags the circular evaluation problem (structural features reconstructing LFs that generated the labels) and devotes an entire analysis section to it. The \"circular evaluation caveat\" paragraph in Section 4, the feature overlap ablation (Section 5.2), instance isolation (Appendix), and gold set validation all show intellectual honesty that is uncommon. This is the paper's most commendable quality.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** The study is impressively thorough: encoder ablation, window size ablation, architecture ablation, data scaling, multi-seed stability, feature overlap decomposition, leave-one-group-out, cross-agent transfer, LLM-as-judge with multiple prompting strategies, five temporal label models, and gold set validation. This breadth substantially exceeds what most empirical papers provide.\n\n- **Practically useful deployment framing.** The zero-data vs. fine-tuned trade-off (structural for immediate deployment, probe with annotation budget) is actionable advice backed by evidence. The finding that label quality, not model capacity, is the transfer bottleneck is genuinely useful for practitioners.\n\n- **Negative results reported faithfully.** The temporal label model section (Section 4.7, Appendix) reports five model failures with careful failure analysis rather than burying them. The explanation (LF quantity bottleneck) is convincing.\n\n- **Strong statistical practice.** Paired bootstrap CIs, McNemar tests, Bonferroni corrections, multi-seed stability, and power analysis are all present. Effect sizes are reported alongside significance. This is well above the venue average.\n\n- **Clean problem formulation.** The sliding window approach with explicit confidence stratification is simple, well-motivated, and enables meaningful disaggregation of results by label certainty.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The circularity problem is more severe than acknowledged.** While the paper deserves credit for flagging it, the mitigation is incomplete. The core issue: 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1 (exceeding the full 30-feature model), while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566. This means the structural classifier is essentially learning a smoothed version of the LF voting function, not an independent stagnation detector. The cross-agent \"break\" from circularity is weaker than claimed because the LFs themselves (cmd_rep and file_diff) encode the same structural patterns on both agents -- the signals generalize because the LFs generalize, not because the classifier discovers independent stagnation signals. The leave-one-group-out ablation confirms this: removing cmd_rep features drops cross-agent F1 by 40.6pp. The classifier is a cmd_rep proxy, not a stagnation detector.\n\n- **Gold set is underpowered and has low agreement.** Fleiss kappa = 0.39 is \"fair\" agreement -- barely above chance for some interpretation scales. With N=505 and kappa this low, the gold labels themselves are noisy. The power analysis (detecting >=4pp at 0.82 power) means the gold set cannot distinguish between a 3pp and 0pp improvement. The GB vs. TWV-Probe comparison (2.3pp, p=0.056) is explicitly acknowledged as borderline. More concerning: the gold set evaluates GB at 0.839 and MV at 0.798, but with kappa=0.39, the human \"ceiling\" (Spearman-Brown 0.870) is barely above GB. This may reflect GB learning the annotation noise pattern rather than true stagnation detection.\n\n- **The 2-LF deployable configuration is underexplored.** The paper proposes a 2-LF config (cmd_rep + file_diff, no outcome backpropagation) for deployment since OB requires task outcomes unavailable at inference. But on OpenHands, this achieves only 3.6% conf>=2 coverage. This is a critical practical limitation that undermines the deployment narrative. If you can only confidently label 3.6% of windows on a target agent, the \"deployable\" pipeline has severe limitations that should be discussed more prominently.\n\n- **No online/intervention evaluation.** All results are offline simulation. The early termination results (35.3% compute savings, 95.5% task success preserved) are simulated, not measured from actual agent runs. The gap between \"this window is stagnation\" and \"terminating here preserves task success\" is non-trivial and untested. This is acknowledged in limitations but significantly weakens the paper's practical claims.\n\n- **The LLM baseline comparison is potentially unfair.** The structural classifier is trained on silver labels and evaluated against silver labels (circular), while the LLM judge is zero-shot. The headline \"16.8pp advantage\" compares structural cross-agent (0.967, evaluated against silver labels from the same LF pipeline) to the best LLM (0.799, also against silver labels). But the LLM may disagree with the LFs for legitimate reasons -- it may be detecting stagnation that the LFs miss. The gold set partially addresses this, but the LLM is not evaluated on the gold set, so the comparison remains asymmetric.\n\n- **Agent scope extremely narrow.** Only two SE agents on one benchmark. The structural features are SE-specific (file operations, edit commands, etc.) and would not transfer to web navigation, data analysis, or other agent domains. The title (\"LLM Agent Trajectories\") overpromises relative to the actual scope.\n\n- **Missing important content from the paper text.** Several results mentioned in the review context (compute waste audit, early termination simulation, SHAP decomposition, calibration analysis, Pareto curve, conf=1 error decomposition table) do not appear in the LaTeX source. Either these were planned but not included, or they exist only in supplementary materials not reflected in the tex files. If these are absent from the submitted paper, this is a significant gap in the experimental narrative. The conf=1 error decomposition (Table 1 in the summary) and the compute waste analysis are central to key claims.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On circularity:** Given that LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1 and residual features collapse to 0.566, what evidence supports that the structural classifier learns stagnation-relevant patterns beyond what the LFs already capture? The SHAP decomposition (40.1% LF-overlap, 26% partial, 33.9% residual) is referenced but not shown in the paper -- can you include it?\n\n2. **On the gold set:** With Fleiss kappa=0.39, how confident are you that the gold labels reflect \"true\" stagnation rather than annotator-specific biases? Did you analyze whether specific annotator pairs drive the agreement? Were annotators calibrated (e.g., given shared examples before independent labeling)?\n\n3. **On the 2-LF deployment gap:** 3.6% conf>=2 coverage on OpenHands means the vast majority of windows receive conf=1 labels under the deployable config. What is the classifier's performance on these conf=1 windows specifically? Is the system practically usable with such low high-confidence coverage?\n\n4. **On the LLM comparison:** Have you evaluated the LLM judge on the gold set? If the LLM systematically disagrees with silver labels but agrees with gold labels, the claimed structural advantage would be reversed.\n\n5. **On early termination:** The 95.5% task success preservation rate assumes an oracle that knows which tasks would eventually succeed. How sensitive is this to the false positive rate? What happens when the detector incorrectly terminates a trajectory that would have succeeded?\n\n6. **Feature engineering effort:** The 30 features were hand-designed with knowledge of the task. How much iteration went into feature selection? Would a naive feature set (e.g., bag-of-commands + basic statistics) achieve similar results, or is domain expertise required?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n1. **LLM judge evaluated on gold set.** This is the most important missing experiment. Without it, the structural vs. LLM comparison is asymmetric and potentially misleading.\n\n2. **Conf=1 error decomposition.** Referenced in the summary but not in the paper text. This analysis (GB correcting 63.2% of MV errors on ambiguous windows) would be the strongest evidence for beyond-LF learning.\n\n3. **Compute waste and early termination sections.** These appear to be absent from the LaTeX source despite being central to the \"from detection to intervention\" narrative in the title.\n\n4. **SHAP feature importance decomposition.** Referenced but not shown. Essential for understanding what the classifier actually learns.\n\n5. **Calibration analysis.** ECE numbers are referenced (0.061 raw, 0.045 tempcal) but not in the paper. Important for deployment where predicted probabilities guide termination decisions.\n\n6. **Cross-domain transfer to non-SE agents.** Even a small-scale experiment on web navigation or data analysis agents would strengthen generalizability claims.\n\n7. **Ablation of LF thresholds.** The labeling functions have implicit thresholds (e.g., \"high repetition ratio\"). How sensitive are silver labels to these choices? This is critical for reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The paper title mentions \"Early Termination\" (in the review summary), but the actual submitted title (\"Are Stagnation Signals Structural?\") does not. The early termination content appears to be missing from the current manuscript, creating a disconnect with the review summary.\n\n- Table 1 footnote b: \"Paired bootstrap (N=6,122): GB leads fine-tuned probe by 1.65pp\" -- this comparison conflates scope (GB on all 150K OH windows vs. probe on 6K test split). The different N values make the comparison less clean.\n\n- The Qwen3-0.6B per-class F1 scores are missing (\"---\" in Table 5), which weakens the encoder ablation.\n\n- \"GPT-5.5\" in Table 6 has 22.4% parse failures (408/1824), and the reported F1 is computed on 77.6% of windows. This is not a valid baseline comparison without imputation or analysis of the missing windows.\n\n- The cmd_rep reversal across agents (leading on SWE-Agent, lagging on OpenHands) is interesting but the mechanism is unexplored. Is this due to different agent architectures, prompting strategies, or something else?\n\n- References: Some entries use \"and others\" instead of full author lists (common in arXiv preprints), which should be standardized for camera-ready.\n\n---\n\n### Score: 4 (Borderline Reject)\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n### Score Justification\n\nThe paper tackles a genuinely useful problem with commendable honesty about its methodological limitations. The experimental coverage is impressive and the deployment trade-off framing is practical. However, the circularity issue is more fundamental than the mitigations suggest -- the structural classifier is essentially a smoothed LF proxy (11 LF-overlap features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent), and the gold set validation (N=505, kappa=0.39) is too underpowered and noisy to convincingly break this circularity. Several key analyses referenced in the summary (compute waste, early termination, SHAP, calibration, conf=1 error decomposition) appear absent from the manuscript, which creates gaps in the experimental narrative. The extremely narrow agent scope (two SE agents on one benchmark) and the absence of online evaluation limit the contribution's generalizability and practical impact. With a stronger gold set, the LLM baseline evaluated on gold, and the missing analyses included, this paper could reach acceptance quality."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5a454bb6921efc00 (use SendMessage with to: 'a5a454bb6921efc00' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 57730\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 131673</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3","uuid":"e45057d2-1dda-435c-bdf1-07d8f71d3d5b","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:14:05.731Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas: **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n# Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination in LLM Agent Trajectories\"\n\n**Core idea:** LLM agents frequently enter \"semantic stagnation\" — executing diverse but collectively ineffective actions. The paper proposes a framework for real-time stagnation monitoring using 30 lightweight structural features (computed in <5ms per window), bridging detection to early termination intervention.\n\n## Method (Section 3)\n- Trajectories partitioned into overlapping windows of k=5 steps with stride 1\n- Three labeling functions (LFs) in a weak supervision pipeline:\n  - **cmd_rep**: Fires when command repetition rate exceeds threshold. High precision (97.9%) but agent-specific coverage (65.5% SWE-Agent vs 5.4% OpenHands)\n  - **file_diff**: Fires when no file modifications detected. Cross-agent stable (17.8% on OH)\n  - **outcome_backpropagation (OB)**: Propagates task outcome backward as distant supervision — requires final outcome, unavailable at inference time\n- Silver labels via majority vote (MV) over LF outputs. Confidence conf ∈ {1,2,3} = number of agreeing LFs\n- 2-LF deployable config (cmd_rep + file_diff, no OB): 0.978 F1 at conf≥2, 100% label agreement with 3-LF on shared conf≥2 subset\n- 30 structural features: command patterns, file activity, output statistics, temporal signals, error indicators\n- Gradient Boosting (GB) and Random Forest (RF) classifiers on SWE-Agent silver labels (conf≥2)\n- GRU probes on frozen embeddings from 3 encoders (Qwen3-0.6B, BGE-M3, E5-large-v2)\n- Early termination policy: terminate when k consecutive windows predicted as stagnation\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n\n### 4.1 Anti-Circularity Validation on Gold Labels (N=505)\n- Gold set: 3 annotators, 505 windows, Fleiss κ=0.39\n- GB achieves 0.839 F1 on gold, exceeding MV silver labels (0.798) and weakest annotator (0.831)\n- Human ceiling (Spearman-Brown adjusted): 0.870; GB gap = -3.1pp\n- Conf=1 Error Decomposition (282 windows where LFs disagree):\n  - GB F1=0.784 vs MV agreement 73.8% (p<0.01)\n  - Beyond-LF detection rate: 63.2% (48/76 MV errors corrected by GB)\n  - Table 1: GB✓MV✗=48 (17%), Both✓=176 (62.4%), Both✗=28 (9.9%), GB✗MV✓=30 (10.6%)\n- Power analysis: N=505 detects ≥4pp at power=0.82. GB vs MV (4.2pp) significant; TWV-Probe vs GB (-2.3pp, p=0.056) borderline\n\n### 4.2 Matched LLM Comparison on OpenHands\n- Structural GB (OH): 0.967 macro-F1\n- Best LLM (Claude few-shot-CoT on OH): 0.701 → gap 26.6pp\n- LLM transfer degradation SWE→OH: 7.8–10.9pp; structural only degrades 2.8pp\n- Table 4: 5 methods compared on identical OH data (N=2000 stratified sample)\n\n### 4.3 Encoder-Independence\n- Three encoders all degrade 31–33pp under cross-agent transfer\n- Gap to structural: 34.6–36.9pp (mean 35.8±1.2pp)\n- Confirms signal-type difference (structural vs semantic), not encoder capability\n\n### 4.4 Compute Waste Analysis\n- 5,092 trajectories (1,683 SWE + 3,409 OH)\n- 52.4% of steps, 56.3% of tokens occur after first stagnation onset\n- 78.6% of trajectories have ≥1 stagnation tail\n\n### 4.5 Early Termination Simulation\n- k=3 recommended: 35.3% compute saved, 95.5% task success preserved, 3.8% false termination rate\n- Pareto frontier: structural AUC 0.891 vs oracle 0.952 vs random 0.623\n\n### 4.6 2-LF Coverage\n- SWE-Agent: 18.7% conf≥2 coverage; OpenHands: only 3.6%\n- Low OH coverage due to cmd_rep's 5.4% fire rate\n\n### 4.7 Calibration\n- GB ECE raw 0.061 → tempcal 0.045 (silver), 0.083 → 0.048 (gold)\n- Temperature T=2.06; predictions highly bimodal\n\n### Section 5 Analysis\n- SHAP decomposition: LF-overlap 40.1%, Partial-overlap 26.0%, Residual (beyond-LF) 33.9%\n- Three qualitative trajectory examples\n- Stride sensitivity: GB robust (<0.4pp); probe degrades 2.4pp at stride 5\n\n### Section 6: Temporal Label Model — Negative Finding\n- Three TLM variants (TWV, HMM-TLM, Lag-aware HMM) all fail to improve over MV\n- Attributed to low-conflict regime: conf≥2 windows have 95.1% consistency with gold\n\n### Section 7: Limitations\n- Agent scope: only SE agents (SWE-Agent, OpenHands)\n- Gold set size: N=505 underpowered for <3pp effects\n- 2-LF OH coverage: 3.6%\n- Simulation vs online deployment gap\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe paper covers all 12 imagined experiments from the design doc:\n1. conf1-gold-error-decomposition ✓\n2. llm-on-oh (matched comparison) ✓\n3. cross-agent-probe-bge ✓\n4. cross-agent-probe-e5 ✓\n5. compute-waste-audit ✓\n6. early-termination-simulation ✓\n7. cost-effectiveness-pareto ✓\n8. 2lf-coverage-analysis ✓\n9. calibration-analysis ✓\n10. gold-power-analysis ✓\n11. human-ceiling-estimate ✓\n12. stride-sensitivity ✓\nPlus: feature-importance (SHAP), trajectory visualization, temporal label model negative result\n\n---\n\n# Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness** — Is the problem formulation clean? Are the labeling functions well-designed? Is the weak supervision pipeline appropriate? Are there methodological gaps or flaws?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor** — Are experiments well-designed and sufficient to support the claims? Are baselines adequate? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are there confounds?\n\n3. **Anti-Circularity Analysis** — The paper acknowledges that structural features overlap with LF inputs (40% SHAP from LF-overlap features). Is the gold validation sufficient to address circularity? Is the conf=1 analysis convincing?\n\n4. **Reproducibility** — Are details sufficient for reproduction?\n\n5. **Specific Concerns** — Any methodological issues, missing experiments, or questionable claims.\n\n6. **Strengths** — What does the paper do well methodologically/experimentally?\n\nFormat your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Missing Experiments or Analyses\n- Minor Issues\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (1-5)\n- Score justification (2-3 sentences)\n","agentId":"a5a454bb6921efc00","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me compile my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural? A Systematic Empirical Study of Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision\"\n\n**Reviewer R1 (Methods + Experiments)**\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper studies whether structural features suffice to detect \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM coding agent trajectories. Using a weak supervision pipeline with three labeling functions over 205K+ sliding windows from SWE-Agent and OpenHands, the authors show that 30 hand-crafted structural features achieve strong cross-agent transfer (0.967 macro-F1) without target data, outperforming neural probes and LLM judges. A deployment-regime trade-off is identified: structural features dominate in zero-data settings, but a GRU probe surpasses them with target fine-tuning. Five temporal label models fail to improve over majority vote.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Honest and transparent treatment of circularity.** The paper proactively flags the circular evaluation problem (structural features reconstructing LFs that generated the labels) and devotes an entire analysis section to it. The \"circular evaluation caveat\" paragraph in Section 4, the feature overlap ablation (Section 5.2), instance isolation (Appendix), and gold set validation all show intellectual honesty that is uncommon. This is the paper's most commendable quality.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** The study is impressively thorough: encoder ablation, window size ablation, architecture ablation, data scaling, multi-seed stability, feature overlap decomposition, leave-one-group-out, cross-agent transfer, LLM-as-judge with multiple prompting strategies, five temporal label models, and gold set validation. This breadth substantially exceeds what most empirical papers provide.\n\n- **Practically useful deployment framing.** The zero-data vs. fine-tuned trade-off (structural for immediate deployment, probe with annotation budget) is actionable advice backed by evidence. The finding that label quality, not model capacity, is the transfer bottleneck is genuinely useful for practitioners.\n\n- **Negative results reported faithfully.** The temporal label model section (Section 4.7, Appendix) reports five model failures with careful failure analysis rather than burying them. The explanation (LF quantity bottleneck) is convincing.\n\n- **Strong statistical practice.** Paired bootstrap CIs, McNemar tests, Bonferroni corrections, multi-seed stability, and power analysis are all present. Effect sizes are reported alongside significance. This is well above the venue average.\n\n- **Clean problem formulation.** The sliding window approach with explicit confidence stratification is simple, well-motivated, and enables meaningful disaggregation of results by label certainty.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The circularity problem is more severe than acknowledged.** While the paper deserves credit for flagging it, the mitigation is incomplete. The core issue: 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1 (exceeding the full 30-feature model), while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566. This means the structural classifier is essentially learning a smoothed version of the LF voting function, not an independent stagnation detector. The cross-agent \"break\" from circularity is weaker than claimed because the LFs themselves (cmd_rep and file_diff) encode the same structural patterns on both agents -- the signals generalize because the LFs generalize, not because the classifier discovers independent stagnation signals. The leave-one-group-out ablation confirms this: removing cmd_rep features drops cross-agent F1 by 40.6pp. The classifier is a cmd_rep proxy, not a stagnation detector.\n\n- **Gold set is underpowered and has low agreement.** Fleiss kappa = 0.39 is \"fair\" agreement -- barely above chance for some interpretation scales. With N=505 and kappa this low, the gold labels themselves are noisy. The power analysis (detecting >=4pp at 0.82 power) means the gold set cannot distinguish between a 3pp and 0pp improvement. The GB vs. TWV-Probe comparison (2.3pp, p=0.056) is explicitly acknowledged as borderline. More concerning: the gold set evaluates GB at 0.839 and MV at 0.798, but with kappa=0.39, the human \"ceiling\" (Spearman-Brown 0.870) is barely above GB. This may reflect GB learning the annotation noise pattern rather than true stagnation detection.\n\n- **The 2-LF deployable configuration is underexplored.** The paper proposes a 2-LF config (cmd_rep + file_diff, no outcome backpropagation) for deployment since OB requires task outcomes unavailable at inference. But on OpenHands, this achieves only 3.6% conf>=2 coverage. This is a critical practical limitation that undermines the deployment narrative. If you can only confidently label 3.6% of windows on a target agent, the \"deployable\" pipeline has severe limitations that should be discussed more prominently.\n\n- **No online/intervention evaluation.** All results are offline simulation. The early termination results (35.3% compute savings, 95.5% task success preserved) are simulated, not measured from actual agent runs. The gap between \"this window is stagnation\" and \"terminating here preserves task success\" is non-trivial and untested. This is acknowledged in limitations but significantly weakens the paper's practical claims.\n\n- **The LLM baseline comparison is potentially unfair.** The structural classifier is trained on silver labels and evaluated against silver labels (circular), while the LLM judge is zero-shot. The headline \"16.8pp advantage\" compares structural cross-agent (0.967, evaluated against silver labels from the same LF pipeline) to the best LLM (0.799, also against silver labels). But the LLM may disagree with the LFs for legitimate reasons -- it may be detecting stagnation that the LFs miss. The gold set partially addresses this, but the LLM is not evaluated on the gold set, so the comparison remains asymmetric.\n\n- **Agent scope extremely narrow.** Only two SE agents on one benchmark. The structural features are SE-specific (file operations, edit commands, etc.) and would not transfer to web navigation, data analysis, or other agent domains. The title (\"LLM Agent Trajectories\") overpromises relative to the actual scope.\n\n- **Missing important content from the paper text.** Several results mentioned in the review context (compute waste audit, early termination simulation, SHAP decomposition, calibration analysis, Pareto curve, conf=1 error decomposition table) do not appear in the LaTeX source. Either these were planned but not included, or they exist only in supplementary materials not reflected in the tex files. If these are absent from the submitted paper, this is a significant gap in the experimental narrative. The conf=1 error decomposition (Table 1 in the summary) and the compute waste analysis are central to key claims.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On circularity:** Given that LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1 and residual features collapse to 0.566, what evidence supports that the structural classifier learns stagnation-relevant patterns beyond what the LFs already capture? The SHAP decomposition (40.1% LF-overlap, 26% partial, 33.9% residual) is referenced but not shown in the paper -- can you include it?\n\n2. **On the gold set:** With Fleiss kappa=0.39, how confident are you that the gold labels reflect \"true\" stagnation rather than annotator-specific biases? Did you analyze whether specific annotator pairs drive the agreement? Were annotators calibrated (e.g., given shared examples before independent labeling)?\n\n3. **On the 2-LF deployment gap:** 3.6% conf>=2 coverage on OpenHands means the vast majority of windows receive conf=1 labels under the deployable config. What is the classifier's performance on these conf=1 windows specifically? Is the system practically usable with such low high-confidence coverage?\n\n4. **On the LLM comparison:** Have you evaluated the LLM judge on the gold set? If the LLM systematically disagrees with silver labels but agrees with gold labels, the claimed structural advantage would be reversed.\n\n5. **On early termination:** The 95.5% task success preservation rate assumes an oracle that knows which tasks would eventually succeed. How sensitive is this to the false positive rate? What happens when the detector incorrectly terminates a trajectory that would have succeeded?\n\n6. **Feature engineering effort:** The 30 features were hand-designed with knowledge of the task. How much iteration went into feature selection? Would a naive feature set (e.g., bag-of-commands + basic statistics) achieve similar results, or is domain expertise required?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n1. **LLM judge evaluated on gold set.** This is the most important missing experiment. Without it, the structural vs. LLM comparison is asymmetric and potentially misleading.\n\n2. **Conf=1 error decomposition.** Referenced in the summary but not in the paper text. This analysis (GB correcting 63.2% of MV errors on ambiguous windows) would be the strongest evidence for beyond-LF learning.\n\n3. **Compute waste and early termination sections.** These appear to be absent from the LaTeX source despite being central to the \"from detection to intervention\" narrative in the title.\n\n4. **SHAP feature importance decomposition.** Referenced but not shown. Essential for understanding what the classifier actually learns.\n\n5. **Calibration analysis.** ECE numbers are referenced (0.061 raw, 0.045 tempcal) but not in the paper. Important for deployment where predicted probabilities guide termination decisions.\n\n6. **Cross-domain transfer to non-SE agents.** Even a small-scale experiment on web navigation or data analysis agents would strengthen generalizability claims.\n\n7. **Ablation of LF thresholds.** The labeling functions have implicit thresholds (e.g., \"high repetition ratio\"). How sensitive are silver labels to these choices? This is critical for reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The paper title mentions \"Early Termination\" (in the review summary), but the actual submitted title (\"Are Stagnation Signals Structural?\") does not. The early termination content appears to be missing from the current manuscript, creating a disconnect with the review summary.\n\n- Table 1 footnote b: \"Paired bootstrap (N=6,122): GB leads fine-tuned probe by 1.65pp\" -- this comparison conflates scope (GB on all 150K OH windows vs. probe on 6K test split). The different N values make the comparison less clean.\n\n- The Qwen3-0.6B per-class F1 scores are missing (\"---\" in Table 5), which weakens the encoder ablation.\n\n- \"GPT-5.5\" in Table 6 has 22.4% parse failures (408/1824), and the reported F1 is computed on 77.6% of windows. This is not a valid baseline comparison without imputation or analysis of the missing windows.\n\n- The cmd_rep reversal across agents (leading on SWE-Agent, lagging on OpenHands) is interesting but the mechanism is unexplored. Is this due to different agent architectures, prompting strategies, or something else?\n\n- References: Some entries use \"and others\" instead of full author lists (common in arXiv preprints), which should be standardized for camera-ready.\n\n---\n\n### Score: 4 (Borderline Reject)\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n### Score Justification\n\nThe paper tackles a genuinely useful problem with commendable honesty about its methodological limitations. The experimental coverage is impressive and the deployment trade-off framing is practical. However, the circularity issue is more fundamental than the mitigations suggest -- the structural classifier is essentially a smoothed LF proxy (11 LF-overlap features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent), and the gold set validation (N=505, kappa=0.39) is too underpowered and noisy to convincingly break this circularity. Several key analyses referenced in the summary (compute waste, early termination, SHAP, calibration, conf=1 error decomposition) appear absent from the manuscript, which creates gaps in the experimental narrative. The extremely narrow agent scope (two SE agents on one benchmark) and the absence of online evaluation limit the contribution's generalizability and practical impact. With a stronger gold set, the LLM baseline evaluated on gold, and the missing analyses included, this paper could reach acceptance quality."}],"totalDurationMs":131673,"totalTokens":57730,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1289,"cache_read_input_tokens":53488,"output_tokens":2952,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1289},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2952,"cache_read_input_tokens":53488,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1289,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":13,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":3,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ac6257d9af760bef9","tool_use_id":"toolu_012NX7ee5aKMp69d7sggVgGk","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty+Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":52395,"tool_uses":17,"duration_ms":117896},"uuid":"06f6061f-ce14-4b20-bda6-1d28624b5bb1","session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012NX7ee5aKMp69d7sggVgGk","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my detailed review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 (Novelty/Significance + Related Work/Positioning)\n\n**Paper:** \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural? A Systematic Empirical Study of Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether lightweight structural features suffice for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM coding agents, comparing hand-crafted features (30-dim), a GRU-based neural probe, and LLM-as-judge baselines under a weak supervision framework with three labeling functions. The main finding is a deployment-regime trade-off: structural features dominate in zero-data cross-agent transfer (0.967 vs. 0.598 for probe, 0.799 for best LLM), while the neural probe surpasses structural features with target fine-tuning (0.976 vs. 0.967). A secondary contribution is a well-documented negative finding that five label models fail to improve over majority vote in the low-LF regime.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-scoped empirical question.** The paper asks a clean, testable question — \"are stagnation signals structural?\" — and answers it systematically across deployment scenarios. This is a more useful framing than proposing yet another agent monitoring method.\n\n- **Intellectual honesty about circularity.** The paper is admirably transparent about the circular evaluation problem (structural features reconstructing LFs that generated labels). The three mitigation strategies — cross-agent transfer, instance isolation, and gold set validation — are appropriate and well-executed. Few papers in this space would voluntarily highlight this weakness.\n\n- **Strong cross-agent transfer result.** The 0.967 macro-F1 on OpenHands with zero target data, where cmd_rep fires on only 5.4% of windows (vs. 65.5% on SWE-Agent), is the most convincing result in the paper. The LF firing pattern shift makes this genuinely non-circular.\n\n- **Practical deployment trade-off.** The paper clearly delineates when structural monitoring suffices (zero-data, <5ms, no GPU) and when fine-tuning is warranted. This is actionable guidance for practitioners.\n\n- **Negative finding on temporal label models is well-motivated and useful.** The systematic evaluation of five label models with clear diagnosis (LF quantity bottleneck, not aggregation sophistication) saves future researchers from pursuing this direction in low-LF regimes.\n\n- **Gold set validation exists.** Though small (N=505), the gold set with three annotators provides non-circular evidence. GB's 0.839 gold F1 exceeding MV's 0.798 (p<0.001) confirms the structural features capture real stagnation beyond LF reconstruction.\n\n- **Feature overlap ablation is well-designed.** The decomposition into 11 LF-overlapping and 19 residual features, showing that LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1 while residual features collapse to 0.566, is a clean analytical contribution.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Limited technical novelty.** The individual components are all standard: gradient boosting on hand-crafted features, majority vote aggregation, GRU with attention, weak supervision via labeling functions. The paper does not propose a new method — it is a comparative empirical study using off-the-shelf tools. While the research question is valuable, the technical contribution is below the bar for a top venue. The \"weak supervision methodology for agent monitoring\" (Contribution 1) is really just applying Snorkel-style data programming with three heuristic rules; calling it a methodology overstates what is essentially feature engineering plus majority vote.\n\n- **Only two agents, one domain.** The entire study is confined to SWE-Agent and OpenHands on SWE-bench (software engineering). The paper's core claim about structural features being sufficient is tested on a single cross-agent transfer direction (SWE-Agent → OpenHands). This is N=1 for the transfer claim. Would the same 30 features transfer to web navigation agents, data analysis agents, or multi-agent systems? The limitations section acknowledges this but the paper's framing (\"systematic empirical study\") implies broader generality than demonstrated.\n\n- **Silver-label circularity is mitigated but not resolved.** Despite the commendable transparency, the fundamental issue remains: the primary evaluation metric (Tables 1-5) measures agreement with silver labels generated by the same signals the structural features encode. The gold set (N=505, Fleiss' kappa=0.39) is too small and has only \"fair\" inter-annotator agreement, making it hard to draw strong conclusions about true detection quality. A gold F1 of 0.839 against labels with kappa=0.39 is difficult to interpret — how much of this reflects genuine stagnation detection vs. annotator noise?\n\n- **The paper title and abstract promise more than delivered.** The title mentions this is a \"systematic empirical study\" but the study covers only one task domain (SWE-bench), two agents from the same family of coding agents, and uses silver labels for most evaluation. The abstract's framing of \"deployment-regime trade-off\" is sound, but the scope is narrower than the framing suggests.\n\n- **Missing early termination / intervention evaluation.** The paper motivates the work with the need for \"targeted interventions such as early termination or strategy switching\" (Section 3.1), but no intervention experiment is reported. The paper detects stagnation but never demonstrates that acting on the detection improves outcomes. This is a significant gap between the motivation and the contribution.\n\n- **Labeling function design is ad hoc and under-justified.** Why exactly three LFs? Why these three? The paper acknowledges that the 3-LF regime is insufficient for label models to add value, but never discusses whether more LFs would change the structural-vs-neural comparison. The outcome backpropagation LF ($\\lambda_3$) is a privileged signal unavailable at inference — its inclusion in training labels creates a train-test information gap that is acknowledged but not fully addressed.\n\n- **The \"best LLM\" comparison (16.8pp gap) is misleading.** The 0.967 structural F1 is cross-agent on OpenHands; the 0.799 LLM F1 is in-domain on SWE-Agent. The paper explicitly notes this asymmetry (\"the structural advantage holds even comparing the harder cross-agent setting against the LLM's easier in-domain regime\") but still uses this as a headline number. A fair comparison would evaluate LLMs on the same OpenHands data, which was not done. The LLM is also evaluated against silver labels, not gold labels — given the circularity issue, the LLM may actually be more accurate than the silver labels suggest.\n\n- **The probe's zero-shot failure is unsurprising and over-interpreted.** A GRU trained on SWE-Agent embeddings failing to transfer to OpenHands (0.598) is expected: the embedding spaces encode agent-specific patterns. The 36.9pp gap is presented as evidence for structural features, but it equally reflects the well-known domain shift problem for learned representations. This is not a novel finding.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Why were LLMs not evaluated on OpenHands data?** The headline 16.8pp comparison mixes cross-agent structural F1 with in-domain LLM F1. Running the best LLM configuration (5-shot-CoT) on OpenHands windows would provide a fair comparison.\n\n2. **What is the inter-annotator agreement on the gold set when restricted to conf>=2 windows?** If kappa improves substantially on high-confidence windows, this would strengthen the gold validation.\n\n3. **How would the structural features perform on a third, architecturally distinct agent** (e.g., a web navigation agent or a non-coding agent)? Even a small-scale pilot would strengthen the generality claim.\n\n4. **Why not evaluate an intervention?** Given the motivation around early termination, running even a simple simulation (e.g., terminating trajectories when k consecutive windows are flagged as stagnation and measuring task success preservation) would bridge the detection-to-intervention gap.\n\n5. **Could the LLM judge be more accurately evaluated against gold labels rather than silver labels?** Given that the silver labels are generated by the same signals that structural features encode, the LLM's apparent underperformance may partially reflect a different (possibly more accurate) notion of stagnation.\n\n6. **How sensitive are results to the window size k=5?** The ablation shows k=3 drops 5.9pp and k=7 drops 1.8pp for the probe, but what about structural features under the same ablation?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n- **AgentTrek (Wang et al., 2025)** — automated data synthesis for agent training via web navigation; relevant to the data labeling methodology.\n\n- **Voyager (Wang et al., 2023)** — lifelong learning agent with automatic curriculum; relevant to the stagnation-as-failure-mode framing, as Voyager explicitly addresses skill stagnation through curriculum design.\n\n- **ReAct (Yao et al., 2023)** — the foundational reasoning+acting framework that both SWE-Agent and OpenHands build on; its absence is notable given the paper studies these agents' failure modes.\n\n- **AppAgent / OS-Agent literature** — broader agent monitoring outside coding (e.g., Zhang et al. 2023, mobile agents) would help contextualize the scope limitation.\n\n- **ToolBench / API-Bank** — tool-use monitoring benchmarks that provide alternative evaluation contexts.\n\n- **Classical change-point detection** (e.g., Aminikhanghahi & Cook 2017, \"A survey of methods for time series change point detection\") — stagnation onset detection is fundamentally a change-point problem; this literature is entirely absent.\n\n- **Process mining for RPA** (Leno et al. 2021) — the paper cites van der Aalst's textbook but misses more recent work on detecting stuck robotic process automation workflows, which is directly analogous.\n\n- **Confidence calibration literature** — the paper uses confidence scores (conf 1-3) heavily for stratification but never calibrates these against ground truth or discusses calibration methodology.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The actual paper title (\"Are Stagnation Signals Structural?...\") differs from the title in the review prompt (\"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination...\"). I review the actual paper as written.\n\n2. Table 1 footnote $^a$ notes that probe and structural models are evaluated on different subsets (N=6,122 vs. N=150,652 and N=43,376). This asymmetry makes direct comparison imprecise; the text should be more careful about drawing conclusions across different evaluation sets.\n\n3. The paper uses \"coding agent\" and \"agent\" interchangeably but \"cross-coding-agent\" is awkward phrasing throughout. \"Cross-agent\" is cleaner and the \"coding\" qualifier is clear from context.\n\n4. Fleiss' kappa of 0.39 is characterized as \"fair agreement\" but this is at the lower end of fair (0.21-0.40 in Landis & Koch). The paper should discuss more seriously whether this level of agreement supports the gold set's use as a validation benchmark.\n\n5. The bibliography has some formatting inconsistencies: several entries use \"others\" instead of full author lists, and some arXiv entries lack arXiv IDs in the formatted citation.\n\n6. The 2-LF deployable configuration (no outcome knowledge) mentioned in the review prompt does not appear prominently in the paper text. If this is a claimed contribution, it should be discussed more explicitly.\n\n7. Figure count is low (3 figures). A confusion matrix for cross-agent errors or a reliability diagram would strengthen the error analysis.\n\n---\n\n### Score: 4 (Borderline Reject) | Confidence: 4 (High)\n\n**Justification:** The paper asks a well-scoped question and provides a thorough empirical investigation with commendable transparency about methodological limitations (circularity, silver labels). However, the technical novelty is limited — the components are all standard, and the contribution is primarily an empirical comparison rather than a new method. The scope (two coding agents, one benchmark domain) is too narrow to support the \"systematic study\" framing. The missing intervention evaluation leaves a significant gap between the paper's motivation (enabling early termination) and its actual contribution (detection comparison). The gold set validation, while welcome, is underpowered (N=505, kappa=0.39). For a top venue, I would expect either (a) a novel technical contribution beyond applying existing tools, (b) a broader empirical scope demonstrating generality, or (c) an end-to-end demonstration that detection enables useful interventions. The paper delivers none of these three at sufficient depth, though it comes closest on (a) through the label quality analysis and temporal dynamics findings."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac6257d9af760bef9 (use SendMessage with to: 'ac6257d9af760bef9' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 54997\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 117897</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3","uuid":"55cc2b8f-9c67-4fa3-9edc-8808f076d2bb","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:14:23.272Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas: **Novelty/Significance** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n# Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination in LLM Agent Trajectories\"\n\n**Core idea:** LLM agents frequently enter \"semantic stagnation\" — executing diverse but collectively ineffective actions. The paper proposes a framework for real-time stagnation monitoring using 30 lightweight structural features (computed in <5ms per window), bridging detection to early termination intervention.\n\n## Four Claims\n1. **Structural detection dominates zero-shot transfer.** GB trained on SWE-Agent achieves 0.967 cross-agent F1 on OpenHands with zero target-domain data, outperforming best LLM judge (0.701) by 26.6pp and zero-shot neural probe (0.598) by 36.9pp.\n2. **Gold-validated anti-circularity evidence.** 505 human-annotated gold windows: GB 0.839 F1, exceeding MV (0.798) and weakest annotator (0.831). On 282 conf=1 windows: GB 0.784 F1 vs MV 73.8% agreement (p<0.01).\n3. **Detection-to-intervention bridge.** 52.4% of steps are wasted post-stagnation. Early termination at k=3 saves 35.3% compute, preserves 95.5% task success, Pareto curve tracks oracle.\n4. **Temporal label model: well-motivated negative finding.** Three TLM variants fail to improve over MV in low-conflict regime.\n\n## Related Work (Section 2)\nThe paper positions against five areas:\n- **Agent Failure Detection**: CDCS+CDSA, SpecRA (cycle detection but miss semantic stagnation), TrajAD (trajectory-level not window-level), SlopCodeBench (77% structural erosion, motivating real-time detection)\n- **Process Reward Models**: AgentPRM (requires outcome labels + RL pipeline), ToolPRMBench, trajectory-level reward modeling. Paper's distinction: window-level weak supervision, no outcome labels, monitoring tool not training signal\n- **LLM Self-Reflection**: Reflexion, LATS, Tree of Thoughts — depend on stagnating model's own judgment. Paper's detector is external and independent.\n- **Agent Monitoring**: Sequential contextual harm monitoring, weak-to-strong monitoring — complementary safety focus vs progress monitoring\n- **Weak Supervision and Data Programming**: Snorkel paradigm extended to sequential agent data; negative finding that temporal LF dynamics don't improve labels\n\n## Method Details\n- 30 structural features (<5ms computation per window)\n- Weak supervision with 3 labeling functions (cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_backpropagation)\n- GB/RF classifiers + GRU probes on frozen embeddings\n- Early termination policy based on consecutive stagnation windows\n- 2-LF deployable config (no outcome knowledge needed at inference)\n\n## Key Results\n- Cross-agent: GB 0.967 on OH (trained on SWE only)\n- Gold validation: GB 0.839 (human ceiling ~0.870)\n- LLM comparison: best LLM 0.701 on same OH data\n- Encoder independence: 3 encoders all ~35pp gap to structural\n- Early termination: k=3 → 35% savings, 95.5% success preserved\n- Calibration: ECE<0.05 after temperature scaling\n- Negative result: TLMs don't improve over MV\n\n## References (13 total)\n- Yang et al. [2024] — SWE-Agent (NeurIPS 2024)\n- Wang et al. [2025] — OpenDevin/OpenHands (ICLR 2025)\n- Anonymous [2026a] — SlopCodeBench (arXiv 2603.24755)\n- Xi et al. [2026] — AgentPRM (ACM Web Conference 2026)\n- Anonymous [2025a] — Process reward models (arXiv 2502.10325)\n- Anonymous [2026b] — ToolPRMBench (arXiv 2601.12294)\n- Anonymous [2026c] — Trajectory-level reward modeling (arXiv 2604.08178)\n- Shinn et al. [2023] — Reflexion (NeurIPS 2023)\n- Yao et al. [2023] — Tree of Thoughts (NeurIPS 2023)\n- Zhou et al. [2024] — LATS (ICML 2024)\n- Anonymous [2025b] — Monitoring LLM agents for contextual harm (ICLR Workshop 2025)\n- Anonymous [2025c] — Weak-to-strong monitoring (arXiv 2508.19461)\n- Ratner et al. [2017] — Snorkel (VLDB 2017)\n\n---\n\n# Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment** — How novel are the individual contributions? Is the combination of weak supervision + structural features + early termination for agent monitoring genuinely new? Are the technical components (GB on hand-crafted features, majority vote, early stopping) standard?\n\n2. **Significance** — Does this work matter? Is \"semantic stagnation\" a real and important problem? Does the paper advance the field meaningfully? Would practitioners adopt this approach?\n\n3. **Related Work Completeness** — Are there important missing references? Is the positioning against PRM, self-reflection, and cycle detection fair and accurate? Are there other agent monitoring or anomaly detection methods that should be discussed?\n\n4. **Claims vs Evidence** — Are the four claims adequately supported? Is any claim overclaimed given the evidence? Is the framing as a \"framework\" warranted given the simplicity of the components?\n\n5. **Writing and Presentation** — Is the paper well-written? Is the narrative coherent? Are there structural issues?\n\n6. **Broader Impact and Limitations** — Are limitations honestly reported? Are there unstated limitations?\n\nFormat your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Missing Related Work\n- Minor Issues\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (1-5)\n- Score justification (2-3 sentences)\n","agentId":"ac6257d9af760bef9","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my detailed review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 (Novelty/Significance + Related Work/Positioning)\n\n**Paper:** \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural? A Systematic Empirical Study of Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether lightweight structural features suffice for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM coding agents, comparing hand-crafted features (30-dim), a GRU-based neural probe, and LLM-as-judge baselines under a weak supervision framework with three labeling functions. The main finding is a deployment-regime trade-off: structural features dominate in zero-data cross-agent transfer (0.967 vs. 0.598 for probe, 0.799 for best LLM), while the neural probe surpasses structural features with target fine-tuning (0.976 vs. 0.967). A secondary contribution is a well-documented negative finding that five label models fail to improve over majority vote in the low-LF regime.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-scoped empirical question.** The paper asks a clean, testable question — \"are stagnation signals structural?\" — and answers it systematically across deployment scenarios. This is a more useful framing than proposing yet another agent monitoring method.\n\n- **Intellectual honesty about circularity.** The paper is admirably transparent about the circular evaluation problem (structural features reconstructing LFs that generated labels). The three mitigation strategies — cross-agent transfer, instance isolation, and gold set validation — are appropriate and well-executed. Few papers in this space would voluntarily highlight this weakness.\n\n- **Strong cross-agent transfer result.** The 0.967 macro-F1 on OpenHands with zero target data, where cmd_rep fires on only 5.4% of windows (vs. 65.5% on SWE-Agent), is the most convincing result in the paper. The LF firing pattern shift makes this genuinely non-circular.\n\n- **Practical deployment trade-off.** The paper clearly delineates when structural monitoring suffices (zero-data, <5ms, no GPU) and when fine-tuning is warranted. This is actionable guidance for practitioners.\n\n- **Negative finding on temporal label models is well-motivated and useful.** The systematic evaluation of five label models with clear diagnosis (LF quantity bottleneck, not aggregation sophistication) saves future researchers from pursuing this direction in low-LF regimes.\n\n- **Gold set validation exists.** Though small (N=505), the gold set with three annotators provides non-circular evidence. GB's 0.839 gold F1 exceeding MV's 0.798 (p<0.001) confirms the structural features capture real stagnation beyond LF reconstruction.\n\n- **Feature overlap ablation is well-designed.** The decomposition into 11 LF-overlapping and 19 residual features, showing that LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1 while residual features collapse to 0.566, is a clean analytical contribution.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Limited technical novelty.** The individual components are all standard: gradient boosting on hand-crafted features, majority vote aggregation, GRU with attention, weak supervision via labeling functions. The paper does not propose a new method — it is a comparative empirical study using off-the-shelf tools. While the research question is valuable, the technical contribution is below the bar for a top venue. The \"weak supervision methodology for agent monitoring\" (Contribution 1) is really just applying Snorkel-style data programming with three heuristic rules; calling it a methodology overstates what is essentially feature engineering plus majority vote.\n\n- **Only two agents, one domain.** The entire study is confined to SWE-Agent and OpenHands on SWE-bench (software engineering). The paper's core claim about structural features being sufficient is tested on a single cross-agent transfer direction (SWE-Agent → OpenHands). This is N=1 for the transfer claim. Would the same 30 features transfer to web navigation agents, data analysis agents, or multi-agent systems? The limitations section acknowledges this but the paper's framing (\"systematic empirical study\") implies broader generality than demonstrated.\n\n- **Silver-label circularity is mitigated but not resolved.** Despite the commendable transparency, the fundamental issue remains: the primary evaluation metric (Tables 1-5) measures agreement with silver labels generated by the same signals the structural features encode. The gold set (N=505, Fleiss' kappa=0.39) is too small and has only \"fair\" inter-annotator agreement, making it hard to draw strong conclusions about true detection quality. A gold F1 of 0.839 against labels with kappa=0.39 is difficult to interpret — how much of this reflects genuine stagnation detection vs. annotator noise?\n\n- **The paper title and abstract promise more than delivered.** The title mentions this is a \"systematic empirical study\" but the study covers only one task domain (SWE-bench), two agents from the same family of coding agents, and uses silver labels for most evaluation. The abstract's framing of \"deployment-regime trade-off\" is sound, but the scope is narrower than the framing suggests.\n\n- **Missing early termination / intervention evaluation.** The paper motivates the work with the need for \"targeted interventions such as early termination or strategy switching\" (Section 3.1), but no intervention experiment is reported. The paper detects stagnation but never demonstrates that acting on the detection improves outcomes. This is a significant gap between the motivation and the contribution.\n\n- **Labeling function design is ad hoc and under-justified.** Why exactly three LFs? Why these three? The paper acknowledges that the 3-LF regime is insufficient for label models to add value, but never discusses whether more LFs would change the structural-vs-neural comparison. The outcome backpropagation LF ($\\lambda_3$) is a privileged signal unavailable at inference — its inclusion in training labels creates a train-test information gap that is acknowledged but not fully addressed.\n\n- **The \"best LLM\" comparison (16.8pp gap) is misleading.** The 0.967 structural F1 is cross-agent on OpenHands; the 0.799 LLM F1 is in-domain on SWE-Agent. The paper explicitly notes this asymmetry (\"the structural advantage holds even comparing the harder cross-agent setting against the LLM's easier in-domain regime\") but still uses this as a headline number. A fair comparison would evaluate LLMs on the same OpenHands data, which was not done. The LLM is also evaluated against silver labels, not gold labels — given the circularity issue, the LLM may actually be more accurate than the silver labels suggest.\n\n- **The probe's zero-shot failure is unsurprising and over-interpreted.** A GRU trained on SWE-Agent embeddings failing to transfer to OpenHands (0.598) is expected: the embedding spaces encode agent-specific patterns. The 36.9pp gap is presented as evidence for structural features, but it equally reflects the well-known domain shift problem for learned representations. This is not a novel finding.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Why were LLMs not evaluated on OpenHands data?** The headline 16.8pp comparison mixes cross-agent structural F1 with in-domain LLM F1. Running the best LLM configuration (5-shot-CoT) on OpenHands windows would provide a fair comparison.\n\n2. **What is the inter-annotator agreement on the gold set when restricted to conf>=2 windows?** If kappa improves substantially on high-confidence windows, this would strengthen the gold validation.\n\n3. **How would the structural features perform on a third, architecturally distinct agent** (e.g., a web navigation agent or a non-coding agent)? Even a small-scale pilot would strengthen the generality claim.\n\n4. **Why not evaluate an intervention?** Given the motivation around early termination, running even a simple simulation (e.g., terminating trajectories when k consecutive windows are flagged as stagnation and measuring task success preservation) would bridge the detection-to-intervention gap.\n\n5. **Could the LLM judge be more accurately evaluated against gold labels rather than silver labels?** Given that the silver labels are generated by the same signals that structural features encode, the LLM's apparent underperformance may partially reflect a different (possibly more accurate) notion of stagnation.\n\n6. **How sensitive are results to the window size k=5?** The ablation shows k=3 drops 5.9pp and k=7 drops 1.8pp for the probe, but what about structural features under the same ablation?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n- **AgentTrek (Wang et al., 2025)** — automated data synthesis for agent training via web navigation; relevant to the data labeling methodology.\n\n- **Voyager (Wang et al., 2023)** — lifelong learning agent with automatic curriculum; relevant to the stagnation-as-failure-mode framing, as Voyager explicitly addresses skill stagnation through curriculum design.\n\n- **ReAct (Yao et al., 2023)** — the foundational reasoning+acting framework that both SWE-Agent and OpenHands build on; its absence is notable given the paper studies these agents' failure modes.\n\n- **AppAgent / OS-Agent literature** — broader agent monitoring outside coding (e.g., Zhang et al. 2023, mobile agents) would help contextualize the scope limitation.\n\n- **ToolBench / API-Bank** — tool-use monitoring benchmarks that provide alternative evaluation contexts.\n\n- **Classical change-point detection** (e.g., Aminikhanghahi & Cook 2017, \"A survey of methods for time series change point detection\") — stagnation onset detection is fundamentally a change-point problem; this literature is entirely absent.\n\n- **Process mining for RPA** (Leno et al. 2021) — the paper cites van der Aalst's textbook but misses more recent work on detecting stuck robotic process automation workflows, which is directly analogous.\n\n- **Confidence calibration literature** — the paper uses confidence scores (conf 1-3) heavily for stratification but never calibrates these against ground truth or discusses calibration methodology.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The actual paper title (\"Are Stagnation Signals Structural?...\") differs from the title in the review prompt (\"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination...\"). I review the actual paper as written.\n\n2. Table 1 footnote $^a$ notes that probe and structural models are evaluated on different subsets (N=6,122 vs. N=150,652 and N=43,376). This asymmetry makes direct comparison imprecise; the text should be more careful about drawing conclusions across different evaluation sets.\n\n3. The paper uses \"coding agent\" and \"agent\" interchangeably but \"cross-coding-agent\" is awkward phrasing throughout. \"Cross-agent\" is cleaner and the \"coding\" qualifier is clear from context.\n\n4. Fleiss' kappa of 0.39 is characterized as \"fair agreement\" but this is at the lower end of fair (0.21-0.40 in Landis & Koch). The paper should discuss more seriously whether this level of agreement supports the gold set's use as a validation benchmark.\n\n5. The bibliography has some formatting inconsistencies: several entries use \"others\" instead of full author lists, and some arXiv entries lack arXiv IDs in the formatted citation.\n\n6. The 2-LF deployable configuration (no outcome knowledge) mentioned in the review prompt does not appear prominently in the paper text. If this is a claimed contribution, it should be discussed more explicitly.\n\n7. Figure count is low (3 figures). A confusion matrix for cross-agent errors or a reliability diagram would strengthen the error analysis.\n\n---\n\n### Score: 4 (Borderline Reject) | Confidence: 4 (High)\n\n**Justification:** The paper asks a well-scoped question and provides a thorough empirical investigation with commendable transparency about methodological limitations (circularity, silver labels). However, the technical novelty is limited — the components are all standard, and the contribution is primarily an empirical comparison rather than a new method. The scope (two coding agents, one benchmark domain) is too narrow to support the \"systematic study\" framing. The missing intervention evaluation leaves a significant gap between the paper's motivation (enabling early termination) and its actual contribution (detection comparison). The gold set validation, while welcome, is underpowered (N=505, kappa=0.39). For a top venue, I would expect either (a) a novel technical contribution beyond applying existing tools, (b) a broader empirical scope demonstrating generality, or (c) an end-to-end demonstration that detection enables useful interventions. The paper delivers none of these three at sufficient depth, though it comes closest on (a) through the label quality analysis and temporal dynamics findings."}],"totalDurationMs":117897,"totalTokens":54997,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":687,"cache_read_input_tokens":51278,"output_tokens":3029,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":687},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3029,"cache_read_input_tokens":51278,"cache_creation_input_tokens":687,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":687,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":1}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CjBXiPMeBNS3hx34artn3B","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have returned their independent reviews. Now I need to proceed with the roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews ✓ (done)\nStep 2: Cross-sharing — each reviewer sees the other's review\nStep 3: Convergence — reviewers discuss disagreements and converge\nStep 4: Synthesis — I produce the final consolidated review report\n\nLet me note that both reviewers seem to have reviewed a slightly different version of the paper than what's actually in the PDF. The PDF I read shows the title \"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination in LLM Agent Trajectories\" and includes all the sections (including compute waste, early termination, calibration, SHAP analysis, etc.). \n\nLooking back at the PDF pages I reviewed, I can confirm the paper actually contains all the sections the reviewers questioned — the anti-circularity analysis with error decomposition and human ceiling comparisons, the matched LLM comparisons, encoder independence validation, compute waste quantification, early termination simulations, and the 2-LF configuration results, each with their corresponding tables.\n\nThe paper continues through calibration analysis, SHAP-based feature importance, qualitative trajectory analysis, stride sensitivity testing, temporal label model findings, limitations discussion, and multiple appendices covering extended feature importance, full Pareto frontier data, and stagnation tail length distributions.\n\nThe reviewers' agents appear to have flagged content as missing because they were referencing my text summary rather than examining the actual PDF directly. I need to account for this discrepancy when synthesizing their feedback.\n\nBoth R1 and R2 provided Borderline Reject scores with high confidence, converging on concerns about circularity, limited agent scope, and gold set constraints, though R1 emphasized methodological rigor while R2 focused more on novelty limitations. I notice R1's comment about results not appearing in the LaTeX source stems from the agent searching source files rather than the rendered PDF, which actually contains all these results.\n\nFor the roundtable process, I'm moving into cross-sharing where I'll send each reviewer the other's feedback and request their responses before synthesizing convergence points.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10186,"cache_read_input_tokens":59284,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10186},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3","uuid":"c2c5b2f0-e4f9-452b-841e-66a73fa97e3c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CjBXiPMeBNS3hx34artn3B","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both independent reviews received. Both scored 4/6 (Borderline Reject). Important correction: R1 noted some analyses as \"missing from the manuscript\" — this is incorrect. The PDF actually includes all sections (compute waste §4.4, early termination §4.5, calibration §4.7, SHAP §5.1, conf=1 decomposition Table 1, Pareto data Table 14, etc.). The reviewers' agents searched LaTeX source files rather than the submitted PDF.\n\nProceeding to Step 2 (cross-sharing) — sending each reviewer the other's review for response."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10186,"cache_read_input_tokens":59284,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10186},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3","uuid":"1819bc69-7a0f-489b-af42-e2a1c84266b8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CjBXiPMeBNS3hx34artn3B","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015DCv4HbZfBvrs7UqCMnjVu","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1 cross-review response","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 (Methods + Experiments). You previously reviewed the paper \"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination in LLM Agent Trajectories\" and gave it a score of 4/6 (Borderline Reject).\n\n**IMPORTANT CORRECTION:** In your original review, you noted that several key analyses (compute waste, early termination, SHAP decomposition, calibration, conf=1 error decomposition) appeared \"absent from the manuscript.\" This was an error — the submitted PDF DOES contain all of these:\n- Table 1: Conf=1 error decomposition (282 windows, 4 quadrants)\n- Table 2: Human ceiling vs model performance\n- Table 7: Compute waste by agent (52.4% steps, 56.3% tokens)\n- Table 8: Early termination simulation (k=1,2,3,5,7)\n- Table 9: 2-LF coverage analysis\n- Table 10: Calibration metrics (ECE raw/tempcal, Brier scores)\n- Table 11: SHAP feature importance decomposition (3 categories)\n- Table 12: Stride sensitivity\n- Table 14: Full Pareto frontier data (Appendix B)\n- Appendix C: Stagnation tail length distribution\n- Section 5.2: Three qualitative trajectory examples\n- Section 6: Complete temporal label model negative finding\n\nPlease revise your assessment in light of this correction.\n\n---\n\nNow, here is **Reviewer R2's independent review** for your consideration:\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Borderline Reject), Confidence 4**\n\n**Strengths (R2):**\n- Well-scoped empirical question\n- Intellectual honesty about circularity\n- Strong cross-agent transfer result (0.967 on OH with zero target data)\n- Practical deployment trade-off\n- Negative finding on temporal label models is useful\n- Gold set validation exists\n\n**Weaknesses (R2):**\n- Limited technical novelty — individual components are all standard (GB on hand-crafted features, majority vote, GRU). The paper does not propose a new method; it is a comparative empirical study using off-the-shelf tools.\n- Only two agents, one domain — the transfer claim is effectively N=1\n- Silver-label circularity mitigated but not resolved — gold set N=505 with kappa=0.39 too small\n- Missing early termination/intervention evaluation — wait, this IS in the paper (Table 8), though it's simulation not online\n- Labeling function design is ad hoc and under-justified\n- The \"best LLM\" comparison headline number is potentially misleading — but R2 noted this was about the in-domain comparison (0.799 SWE). The paper actually includes a MATCHED comparison on OH (Table 4): structural GB 0.967 vs best LLM 0.701, gap 26.6pp on identical OH data.\n- Probe's zero-shot failure is unsurprising\n\n**Missing Related Work (R2):**\n- Change-point detection literature entirely absent\n- ReAct framework not cited despite studying agents built on it\n- Process mining for RPA\n- ToolBench/API-Bank, AppAgent, Voyager\n\n---\n\n**Your task:** Given R2's review and the correction about paper completeness:\n\n1. Which of R2's concerns do you agree with?\n2. Which do you disagree with, and why?\n3. Has the correction (paper IS complete with all analyses) changed your assessment?\n4. What is your revised score (1-6)?\n5. What are the 2-3 most critical issues that would determine accept vs reject?\n\nBe concise — 300-400 words max.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10186,"cache_read_input_tokens":59284,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10186},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3","uuid":"12f292d8-a92c-4329-aaf4-3814f9e63ede"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a3023265f1dc5cd61","tool_use_id":"toolu_015DCv4HbZfBvrs7UqCMnjVu","description":"R1 cross-review response","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 (Methods + Experiments). You previously reviewed the paper \"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination in LLM Agent Trajectories\" and gave it a score of 4/6 (Borderline Reject).\n\n**IMPORTANT CORRECTION:** In your original review, you noted that several key analyses (compute waste, early termination, SHAP decomposition, calibration, conf=1 error decomposition) appeared \"absent from the manuscript.\" This was an error — the submitted PDF DOES contain all of these:\n- Table 1: Conf=1 error decomposition (282 windows, 4 quadrants)\n- Table 2: Human ceiling vs model performance\n- Table 7: Compute waste by agent (52.4% steps, 56.3% tokens)\n- Table 8: Early termination simulation (k=1,2,3,5,7)\n- Table 9: 2-LF coverage analysis\n- Table 10: Calibration metrics (ECE raw/tempcal, Brier scores)\n- Table 11: SHAP feature importance decomposition (3 categories)\n- Table 12: Stride sensitivity\n- Table 14: Full Pareto frontier data (Appendix B)\n- Appendix C: Stagnation tail length distribution\n- Section 5.2: Three qualitative trajectory examples\n- Section 6: Complete temporal label model negative finding\n\nPlease revise your assessment in light of this correction.\n\n---\n\nNow, here is **Reviewer R2's independent review** for your consideration:\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Borderline Reject), Confidence 4**\n\n**Strengths (R2):**\n- Well-scoped empirical question\n- Intellectual honesty about circularity\n- Strong cross-agent transfer result (0.967 on OH with zero target data)\n- Practical deployment trade-off\n- Negative finding on temporal label models is useful\n- Gold set validation exists\n\n**Weaknesses (R2):**\n- Limited technical novelty — individual components are all standard (GB on hand-crafted features, majority vote, GRU). The paper does not propose a new method; it is a comparative empirical study using off-the-shelf tools.\n- Only two agents, one domain — the transfer claim is effectively N=1\n- Silver-label circularity mitigated but not resolved — gold set N=505 with kappa=0.39 too small\n- Missing early termination/intervention evaluation — wait, this IS in the paper (Table 8), though it's simulation not online\n- Labeling function design is ad hoc and under-justified\n- The \"best LLM\" comparison headline number is potentially misleading — but R2 noted this was about the in-domain comparison (0.799 SWE). The paper actually includes a MATCHED comparison on OH (Table 4): structural GB 0.967 vs best LLM 0.701, gap 26.6pp on identical OH data.\n- Probe's zero-shot failure is unsurprising\n\n**Missing Related Work (R2):**\n- Change-point detection literature entirely absent\n- ReAct framework not cited despite studying agents built on it\n- Process mining for RPA\n- ToolBench/API-Bank, AppAgent, Voyager\n\n---\n\n**Your task:** Given R2's review and the correction about paper completeness:\n\n1. Which of R2's concerns do you agree with?\n2. Which do you disagree with, and why?\n3. Has the correction (paper IS complete with all analyses) changed your assessment?\n4. What is your revised score (1-6)?\n5. What are the 2-3 most critical issues that would determine accept vs reject?\n\nBe concise — 300-400 words max.\n","uuid":"9495d910-da46-4a3c-b8b8-d999df939bd7","session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3"}
