:**\n- Honest treatment of circularity\n- Comprehensive experimental coverage\n- Practically useful deployment framing\n- Negative results reported faithfully\n- Strong statistical practice (bootstrap CIs, McNemar, Bonferroni, power analysis)\n- Clean problem formulation\n\n**Weaknesses (R1):**\n- Circularity more severe than acknowledged: 11 LF-overlapping features achieve 0.977 cross-agent vs 0.566 for 19 residual features → classifier is essentially a smoothed LF proxy\n- Gold set underpowered (N=505, kappa=0.39) — cannot distinguish 3pp vs 0pp improvement\n- 2-LF deployable config has only 3.6% OH coverage — undermines deployment narrative\n- No online/intervention evaluation (simulation only)\n- LLM baseline comparison potentially unfair (but note: matched comparison on same OH data IS in the paper)\n- Agent scope extremely narrow (2 SE agents)\n\n**Key R1 concern:** The structural classifier's success is explained by the fact that LF-overlapping features carry most of the signal (0.977 alone), while residual features collapse (0.566). The cross-agent generalization comes from the LFs generalizing, not independent stagnation detection.\n\n---\n\n**Your task:** Given R1's review and the correction about paper completeness:\n\n1. Which of R1's concerns do you agree with?\n2. Which do you disagree with, and why?\n3. Has the correction (matched LLM comparison on identical OH data IS in the paper, early termination simulation IS included) changed your assessment?\n4. What is your revised score (1-6)?\n5. What are the 2-3 most critical issues that would determine accept vs reject?\n\nBe concise — 300-400 words max.\n","uuid":"ae2ecfd3-e4be-4fd0-a19a-dfbc1d6bb50d","session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3"}
