{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QM75A2yPEPSxtzHuseKCFN","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have the full picture. The correction claims are substantially incorrect. Here is my revised review:\n\n---\n\n## R2 Revised Review\n\n### 1. Agreement with R1\n\nI agree with R1 on the following:\n\n**Circularity is the central weakness.** The feature overlap ablation (Table in Appendix B.12) is damning: LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent, while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566. R1 is correct that the classifier is essentially a smoothed LF proxy. The authors' defense -- that cross-agent transfer \"breaks circularity\" because LF firing patterns differ -- is undermined by the fact that the features generalizing are exactly the LF-overlapping ones. The non-LF features contribute nothing cross-agent.\n\n**Gold set is underpowered.** N=505 with kappa=0.39 is insufficient. The 4.2pp GB advantage over MV (0.839 vs 0.798) is real but modest, and moderate IAA means the gold labels themselves are noisy.\n\n**Agent scope is narrow.** Two SE agents on one benchmark is thin for the paper's generality claims.\n\n### 2. Disagreement with R1\n\n**The LLM comparison IS fair for the matched setting.** Table 1 reports structural GB (0.967) and LLM-as-Judge (0.663 zero-shot) on SWE-Agent data, and Section 4.2 notes the best LLM reaches 0.799 (five-shot-CoT) -- still 16.8pp below structural cross-agent. R1 flagged this as \"potentially unfair\" but it is well-controlled.\n\n**The 2-LF/3.6% coverage issue is less severe than R1 implies.** The paper honestly reports this limitation and frames it as a deployment trade-off, not a solved problem.\n\n### 3. On the Correction\n\nThe correction is **substantially inaccurate**. I re-read the full LaTeX source. The paper does NOT contain:\n- Section 4.4 (compute waste / Table 7)\n- Section 4.5 (early termination simulation / Table 8)\n- Section 4.6 (2-LF deployable config / Table 9)\n- Section 4.7 (calibration / Table 10)\n- Section 5.1 (SHAP decomposition / Table 11)\n- Appendix B Pareto frontier data / Table 14\n- Qualitative trajectory examples\n\nThe actual title is \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural? A Systematic Empirical Study of Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision\" -- NOT the title claimed in the correction. The limitations section explicitly states \"No online evaluation.\" The paper's sections 4.4-4.8 actually cover LF temporal dynamics, cmd_rep ablation, temporal label models, and gold set validation. My original concerns about missing early termination evaluation and downstream application analysis **remain fully valid**.\n\n### 4. Revised Score: 4/6 (Borderline Reject)\n\nScore unchanged. The correction was factually wrong about paper content, so it provides no basis for revision.\n\n### 5. Critical Accept/Reject Issues\n\n1. **The circularity-generalization paradox.** The features that generalize cross-agent are exactly the LF-overlapping ones (0.977 vs 0.566). This means the paper's core contribution -- structural features detect stagnation -- reduces to \"LF-derived features reconstruct LFs across agents.\" The authors need either (a) residual features that generalize, or (b) a much larger gold evaluation proving the structural signal is real beyond LF reconstruction.\n\n2. **No downstream evaluation.** Despite \"early termination\" appearing in no section of this paper, the motivation is deployment. Without any compute-savings simulation, intervention experiment, or even a Pareto analysis of detection threshold vs. task success, the practical value remains speculative. The paper is purely a detection study with no demonstrated utility.\n\n3. **Scale of the gold validation.** With N=505 and kappa=0.39, the gold set cannot resolve whether structural features capture genuine stagnation or merely correlate with LF outputs that happen to approximate stagnation. A 2-3x larger gold set with higher IAA would substantially strengthen the paper."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: adeda846a54d12554 (use SendMessage with to: 'adeda846a54d12554' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 37572\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 88990</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3","uuid":"565223b0-c263-4294-9ba6-3770e1cd73d5","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:16:52.282Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 (Novelty + Related Work). You previously reviewed the paper \"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination in LLM Agent Trajectories\" and gave it a score of 4/6 (Borderline Reject).\n\n**IMPORTANT CORRECTION:** In your original review, you noted the paper was missing early termination/intervention evaluation and several analyses. The submitted PDF actually contains ALL of these:\n- Section 4.2: Matched LLM comparison on IDENTICAL OpenHands data (Table 4) — structural GB 0.967 vs Claude few-shot-CoT 0.701, gap 26.6pp. This IS a fair comparison on the same data.\n- Section 4.4: Compute waste analysis (Table 7) — 52.4% steps wasted post-stagnation\n- Section 4.5: Early termination SIMULATION (Table 8) — k=3 saves 35.3%, preserves 95.5% task success\n- Section 4.6: 2-LF deployable configuration (Table 9) with explicit coverage numbers\n- Section 4.7: Calibration analysis (Table 10) — ECE<0.05 after temperature scaling\n- Section 5.1: SHAP decomposition (Table 11) — LF-overlap 40.1%, residual 33.9%\n- Section 5.2: Three qualitative trajectory examples\n- Appendix B: Full Pareto frontier data (Table 14), AUC 0.891 structural vs 0.952 oracle vs 0.623 random\n- The title IS \"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination\" — early termination is a core part of the paper\n\nSo the paper does include: (a) a fair matched LLM comparison on identical data, (b) early termination simulation (though not online), (c) Pareto analysis, (d) calibration for deployment, and (e) SHAP feature decomposition.\n\n---\n\nNow, here is **Reviewer R1's independent review** for your consideration:\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Borderline Reject), Confidence 4**\n\n**Strengths (R1):**\n- Honest treatment of circularity\n- Comprehensive experimental coverage\n- Practically useful deployment framing\n- Negative results reported faithfully\n- Strong statistical practice (bootstrap CIs, McNemar, Bonferroni, power analysis)\n- Clean problem formulation\n\n**Weaknesses (R1):**\n- Circularity more severe than acknowledged: 11 LF-overlapping features achieve 0.977 cross-agent vs 0.566 for 19 residual features → classifier is essentially a smoothed LF proxy\n- Gold set underpowered (N=505, kappa=0.39) — cannot distinguish 3pp vs 0pp improvement\n- 2-LF deployable config has only 3.6% OH coverage — undermines deployment narrative\n- No online/intervention evaluation (simulation only)\n- LLM baseline comparison potentially unfair (but note: matched comparison on same OH data IS in the paper)\n- Agent scope extremely narrow (2 SE agents)\n\n**Key R1 concern:** The structural classifier's success is explained by the fact that LF-overlapping features carry most of the signal (0.977 alone), while residual features collapse (0.566). The cross-agent generalization comes from the LFs generalizing, not independent stagnation detection.\n\n---\n\n**Your task:** Given R1's review and the correction about paper completeness:\n\n1. Which of R1's concerns do you agree with?\n2. Which do you disagree with, and why?\n3. Has the correction (matched LLM comparison on identical OH data IS in the paper, early termination simulation IS included) changed your assessment?\n4. What is your revised score (1-6)?\n5. What are the 2-3 most critical issues that would determine accept vs reject?\n\nBe concise — 300-400 words max.\n","agentId":"adeda846a54d12554","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have the full picture. The correction claims are substantially incorrect. Here is my revised review:\n\n---\n\n## R2 Revised Review\n\n### 1. Agreement with R1\n\nI agree with R1 on the following:\n\n**Circularity is the central weakness.** The feature overlap ablation (Table in Appendix B.12) is damning: LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent, while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566. R1 is correct that the classifier is essentially a smoothed LF proxy. The authors' defense -- that cross-agent transfer \"breaks circularity\" because LF firing patterns differ -- is undermined by the fact that the features generalizing are exactly the LF-overlapping ones. The non-LF features contribute nothing cross-agent.\n\n**Gold set is underpowered.** N=505 with kappa=0.39 is insufficient. The 4.2pp GB advantage over MV (0.839 vs 0.798) is real but modest, and moderate IAA means the gold labels themselves are noisy.\n\n**Agent scope is narrow.** Two SE agents on one benchmark is thin for the paper's generality claims.\n\n### 2. Disagreement with R1\n\n**The LLM comparison IS fair for the matched setting.** Table 1 reports structural GB (0.967) and LLM-as-Judge (0.663 zero-shot) on SWE-Agent data, and Section 4.2 notes the best LLM reaches 0.799 (five-shot-CoT) -- still 16.8pp below structural cross-agent. R1 flagged this as \"potentially unfair\" but it is well-controlled.\n\n**The 2-LF/3.6% coverage issue is less severe than R1 implies.** The paper honestly reports this limitation and frames it as a deployment trade-off, not a solved problem.\n\n### 3. On the Correction\n\nThe correction is **substantially inaccurate**. I re-read the full LaTeX source. The paper does NOT contain:\n- Section 4.4 (compute waste / Table 7)\n- Section 4.5 (early termination simulation / Table 8)\n- Section 4.6 (2-LF deployable config / Table 9)\n- Section 4.7 (calibration / Table 10)\n- Section 5.1 (SHAP decomposition / Table 11)\n- Appendix B Pareto frontier data / Table 14\n- Qualitative trajectory examples\n\nThe actual title is \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural? A Systematic Empirical Study of Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision\" -- NOT the title claimed in the correction. The limitations section explicitly states \"No online evaluation.\" The paper's sections 4.4-4.8 actually cover LF temporal dynamics, cmd_rep ablation, temporal label models, and gold set validation. My original concerns about missing early termination evaluation and downstream application analysis **remain fully valid**.\n\n### 4. Revised Score: 4/6 (Borderline Reject)\n\nScore unchanged. The correction was factually wrong about paper content, so it provides no basis for revision.\n\n### 5. Critical Accept/Reject Issues\n\n1. **The circularity-generalization paradox.** The features that generalize cross-agent are exactly the LF-overlapping ones (0.977 vs 0.566). This means the paper's core contribution -- structural features detect stagnation -- reduces to \"LF-derived features reconstruct LFs across agents.\" The authors need either (a) residual features that generalize, or (b) a much larger gold evaluation proving the structural signal is real beyond LF reconstruction.\n\n2. **No downstream evaluation.** Despite \"early termination\" appearing in no section of this paper, the motivation is deployment. Without any compute-savings simulation, intervention experiment, or even a Pareto analysis of detection threshold vs. task success, the practical value remains speculative. The paper is purely a detection study with no demonstrated utility.\n\n3. **Scale of the gold validation.** With N=505 and kappa=0.39, the gold set cannot resolve whether structural features capture genuine stagnation or merely correlate with LF outputs that happen to approximate stagnation. A 2-3x larger gold set with higher IAA would substantially strengthen the paper."}],"totalDurationMs":88990,"totalTokens":37572,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":189,"cache_read_input_tokens":36372,"output_tokens":1010,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":189},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1010,"cache_read_input_tokens":36372,"cache_creation_input_tokens":189,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":189,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":9,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
