{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A2w2Woi4KiKdAqpuDVytQu","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/sections/analysis.tex:9:This score therefore represents an upper bound on LF reverse-engineering fidelity (a feature overlap ablation in \\S\\ref{sec:feature_overlap} quantifies this decomposition).\ndocs/paper/sections/analysis.tex:17:We partition the 30 features into 11 \\emph{LF-overlapping} and 19 \\emph{residual} (Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_feature_overlap}).\ndocs/paper/sections/analysis.tex:18:On all OH windows, 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1---exceeding the full model by 1.0\\,pp---the opposite of what leakage would predict; this reverses on conf${\\geq}$2 ($-$0.71\\,pp, n.s.), where residual features aid noise filtering.\ndocs/paper/sections/analysis.tex:19:Residual features collapse to 0.566 cross-agent (0.952 in-domain), confirming agent-specific noise.\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:148:GB achieves 0.839 gold macro-F1 vs.\\ 0.798 for MV silver labels ($+$4.2\\,pp, paired bootstrap $p{<}0.001$), confirming that structural features detect stagnation beyond LF reconstruction.\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:11:\\caption{System overview. (Left) Agent trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k{=}5$ steps. (Center) Three labeling functions vote on each window; majority vote produces silver labels with confidence 1--3. (Right) Three detection approaches compared: structural features (30-dim), NL embedding probe (GRU), and LLM-as-judge (eval only, not trained on silver labels).}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:101:A feature is classified as LF-overlapping if it is computed from the same signal space that any labeling function uses as input (\\S\\ref{sec:feature_overlap}).\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:142:\\caption{Complete structural feature definitions ($N{=}30$). LF Overlap indicates which labeling function's signal space the feature shares: $\\lambda_1$ (command repetition), $\\lambda_2$ (file diff), $\\lambda_3$ (outcome backpropagation), or ``---'' (no overlap). 11 features overlap with at least one LF; 19 are residual.}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:442:\\caption{Feature overlap ablation (GB). In-Dom.\\ = SWE-Agent in-domain macro-F1; Cross-Ag.\\ = SWE-Agent $\\to$ OpenHands zero-shot. Top: on all OH windows, LF-overlap features alone exceed the full model cross-coding-agent ($+$1.0\\,pp; on conf${\\geq}$2, direction reverses by $-$0.71\\,pp); residual features collapse. Bottom: removing each LF-group individually; cmd\\_rep features account for the largest transfer drop ($-$40.6\\,pp).}\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:32:A feature overlap ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:feature_overlap}) confirms that 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-coding-agent F1 on all windows, while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566---the features that generalize encode cross-coding-agent stagnation signals, not LF artifacts.\ndocs/paper/main.aux:470:\\@writefile{lof}{\\contentsline {figure}{\\numberline {1}{\\ignorespaces System overview. (Left) Agent trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k{=}5$ steps. (Center) Three labeling functions vote on each window; majority vote produces silver labels with confidence 1--3. (Right) Three detection approaches compared: structural features (30-dim), NL embedding probe (GRU), and LLM-as-judge (eval only, not trained on silver labels).}}{5}{figure.caption.5}\\protected@file@percent }\ndocs/paper/main.aux:472:\\newlabel{fig:pipeline}{{1}{5}{System overview. (Left) Agent trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k{=}5$ steps. (Center) Three labeling functions vote on each window; majority vote produces silver labels with confidence 1--3. (Right) Three detection approaches compared: structural features (30-dim), NL embedding probe (GRU), and LLM-as-judge (eval only, not trained on silver labels)}{figure.caption.5}{}}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:781:\\newlabel{sec:feature_overlap}{{5.2}{8}{Feature Overlap Ablation}{subsection.5.2}{}}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1280:\\@writefile{lot}{\\contentsline {table}{\\numberline {7}{\\ignorespaces Complete structural feature definitions ($N{=}30$). LF Overlap indicates which labeling function's signal space the feature shares: $\\lambda _1$ (command repetition), $\\lambda _2$ (file diff), $\\lambda _3$ (outcome backpropagation), or ``---'' (no overlap). 11 features overlap with at least one LF; 19 are residual.}}{13}{table.caption.34}\\protected@file@percent }\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1281:\\newlabel{tab:feature_defs}{{7}{13}{Complete structural feature definitions ($N{=}30$). LF Overlap indicates which labeling function's signal space the feature shares: $\\lambda _1$ (command repetition), $\\lambda _2$ (file diff), $\\lambda _3$ (outcome backpropagation), or ``---'' (no overlap). 11 features overlap with at least one LF; 19 are residual}{table.caption.34}{}}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1584:\\newlabel{sec:app_feature_overlap}{{A.16}{17}{Feature Overlap Ablation Table}{subsection.A.16}{}}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1585:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1586:\\newlabel{tab:feature_overlap}{{13}{17}{Feature overlap ablation (GB). In-Dom.\\ = SWE-Agent in-domain macro-F1; Cross-Ag.\\ = SWE-Agent $\\to $ OpenHands zero-shot. Top: on all OH windows, LF-overlap features alone exceed the full model cross-coding-agent ($+$1.0\\,pp; on conf${\\geq }$2, direction reverses by $-$0.71\\,pp); residual features collapse. Bottom: removing each LF-group individually; cmd\\_rep features account for the largest transfer drop ($-$40.6\\,pp)}{table.caption.49}{}}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3","uuid":"d3da8970-3569-41a9-8973-444eeb5c928f","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:17:18.917Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"docs/paper/sections/analysis.tex:9:This score therefore represents an upper bound on LF reverse-engineering fidelit