.\\ = SWE-Agent in-domain macro-F1; Cross-Ag.\\ = SWE-Agent $\\to$ OpenHands zero-shot. Top: on all OH windows, LF-overlap features alone exceed the full model cross-coding-agent ($+$1.0\\,pp; on conf${\\geq}$2, direction reverses by $-$0.71\\,pp); residual features collapse. Bottom: removing each LF-group individually; cmd\\_rep features account for the largest transfer drop ($-$40.6\\,pp).}\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:32:A feature overlap ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:feature_overlap}) confirms that 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-coding-agent F1 on all windows, while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566---the features that generalize encode cross-coding-agent stagnation signals, not LF artifacts.\ndocs/paper/main.aux:470:\\@writefile{lof}{\\contentsline {figure}{\\numberline {1}{\\ignorespaces System overview. (Left) Agent trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k{=}5$ steps. (Center) Three labeling functions vote on each window; majority vote produces silver labels with confidence 1--3. (Right) Three detection approaches compared: structural features (30-dim), NL embedding probe (GRU), and LLM-as-judge (eval only, not trained on silver labels).}}{5}{figure.caption.5}\\protected@file@percent }\ndocs/paper/main.aux:472:\\newlabel{fig:pipeline}{{1}{5}{System overview. (Left) Agent trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k{=}5$ steps. (Center) Three labeling functions vote on each window; majority vote produces silver labels with confidence 1--3. (Right) Three detection approaches compared: structural features (30-dim), NL embedding probe (GRU), and LLM-as-judge (eval only, not trained on silver labels)}{figure.caption.5}{}}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:781:\\newlabel{sec:feature_overlap}{{5.2}{8}{Feature Overlap Ablation}{subsection.5.2}{}}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1280:\\@writefile{lot}{\\contentsline {table}{\\numberline {7}{\\ignorespaces Complete structural feature definitions ($N{=}30$). LF Overlap indicates which labeling function's signal space the feature shares: $\\lambda _1$ (command repetition), $\\lambda _2$ (file diff), $\\lambda _3$ (outcome backpropagation), or ``---'' (no overlap). 11 features overlap with at least one LF; 19 are residual.}}{13}{table.caption.34}\\protected@file@percent }\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1281:\\newlabel{tab:feature_defs}{{7}{13}{Complete structural feature definitions ($N{=}30$). LF Overlap indicates which labeling function's signal space the feature shares: $\\lambda _1$ (command repetition), $\\lambda _2$ (file diff), $\\lambda _3$ (outcome backpropagation), or ``---'' (no overlap). 11 features overlap with at least one LF; 19 are residual}{table.caption.34}{}}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1584:\\newlabel{sec:app_feature_overlap}{{A.16}{17}{Feature Overlap Ablation Table}{subsection.A.16}{}}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1585:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/main.aux:1586:\\newlabel{tab:feature_overlap}{{13}{17}{Feature overlap ablation (GB). In-Dom.\\ = SWE-Agent in-domain macro-F1; Cross-Ag.\\ = SWE-Agent $\\to $ OpenHands zero-shot. Top: on all OH windows, LF-overlap features alone exceed the full model cross-coding-agent ($+$1.0\\,pp; on conf${\\geq }$2, direction reverses by $-$0.71\\,pp); residual features collapse. Bottom: removing each LF-group individually; cmd\\_rep features account for the largest transfer drop ($-$40.6\\,pp)}{table.caption.49}{}}","numLines":18}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MFwSTEhBvFfMnmHXKYWeJ3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now I have confirmed key facts:\n\n1. The feature overlap ablation IS in the paper (LaTeX source and PDF). Both versions of the paper discuss it:\n   - 11 LF-overlapping features: cross-agent 0.977\n   - 19 residual features: cross-agent 0.566\n   - Removing cmd_rep features drops cross-agent by 40.6pp\n\n2. The paper actually has TWO versions floating around:\n   - The LaTeX source files (which appear to be a DIFFERENT, possibly earlier version titled \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural?...\")\n   - The submitted PDF (review_input_db82206b.pdf) titled \"Structural Stagnation Probes: From Detection to Compute-Efficient Early Termination...\" is the version under review and it's complete with all sections including compute waste, early termination, calibration, SHAP, etc.\n\nSo R2's concern about incompleteness was based on reading the LaTeX source, which is a different version entirely. The submitted PDF is the full paper. However, R2's circularity concern still holds merit—the LF-overlap ablation actually strengthens that point about the feature dependencies.\n\nNow I'm synthesizing the final review report. Both reviewers converge on several key issues: the circularity problem is fundamental (LF-overlapping features hit 0.977 cross-agent while residual features drop to 0.566), the gold set is underpowered with only 505 samples and kappa of 0.39, and the scope is limited to just two SE agents with one transfer direction. They both acknowledge the paper's intellectual honesty about limitations and appreciate the comprehensive experimental coverage and practical deployment framing. Where they diverge is that R1 upgraded to a Weak Accept after confirming the paper is complete, while R2 remained at a 4 based on the incorrect assumption about incompleteness.\n\nOn novelty, R2 sees it as insufficient for a top venue while R1 views it as borderline depending on the track. Given that the paper is indeed complete with all claimed experiments present, I'm converging toward a 4-5 range. The circularity issue is genuine, but the paper's transparency about it combined with multiple mitigation strategies—cross-agent validation, gold set analysis, confidence filtering, and SHAP explanations—demonstrates real rigor. The narrow scope and lack of online evaluation remain legitimate concerns, so I'm settling on a 4 (Borderline Reject) with a note that revisions could push it to a 5.\n\nThe core tension is that while the paper is thorough and honest about its limitations, the structural features' success across agents appears rooted in the same signals as the labeling functions themselves, which somewhat undermines the \"beyond-LF\" positioning. Still, the matched comparison showing a 26.6 percentage point gap, the practical value demonstrated through early termination simulation, and the useful negative finding about TLMs all count in its favor. The writing is solid and the experimental work is comprehensive, but the technical novelty remains limited and the circularity paradox is difficult to fully dismiss.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3871,"cache_read_input_tokens":74341,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3871},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a13beb9d-44fb-4973-a348-fa71d68014d3","uuid":"a5c95ae7-28e0-41b2-9d55-850d37de54de"}
